2026-09-11,mac,appkit,P57,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#117 phase 3 landed on AppKit: 10000 rows went from 423 MB to 104 MB, against a 101 MB one-row baseline - the list now costs what an empty one costs. 400 rows 104 MB, 2000 rows 103 MB, so it is flat rather than merely smaller. Scrolling still grows it by about 30 KB a row visited (104 MB to 225 MB over 600 scroll segments) because AppKit retains the views: NSKeyValueDependency 1381 to 25835. The rest-state win is real and the scroll-state limit is measured."
2026-09-11,ios,uikit,P57,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#117 phase 3 on UIKit: 10000 rows went from 449 MB to 170 MB, and 500 rows is also 170 MB - flat, not merely smaller. Measured by the app reading its own MACH_TASK_BASIC_INFO resident size, because the simulator gives no ps for the app inside it; cross-checked against ps on macOS where the two agree to 3 MB."
2026-09-11,mac,appkit,P57,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"scroll-time growth fixed: rowViewForRow constructed a fresh NSTableRowView for every row because it had no identifier, so NSTableView could not recycle it. Live NSTableRowView objects went 30 to 1745 while scrolling; now 31. Scrolling the whole 10000-row list settles at 134 MB instead of climbing past 225 MB. The bug predates the lazy rows - the eager path hid it behind its own 423 MB."
2026-09-16,windows,gtk4,P57,ok,ok,window,default,"#117 lazy rows land on GtkBackend. GtkListBox cannot virtualize (bind_model realizes a widget per model item) so Sources/Gtk/Widgets/ListView.swift hand-writes the GtkListView factory stack; every C function was already visible because Sources/CGtk/header.h is the whole gtk.h. Memory with an eager control taken by dropping the conformance and rebuilding: 1 row 256 MB, 400 rows 266, 10000 rows 265, against eager 257/270/410. Flat: +9 MB from 1 to 10000 against +153 eager. The lazy 10000 figure sits ABOVE its own one-row baseline, which is what says rows are really built rather than the list being empty. Pointer replay of P57-lazy-list-gtk4.csv, 25 actions: selection=1 then selection=10 at the SAME y after six wheel notches (so it scrolled), revision=1, rows=1 then 10000, selection=9999 outside the realized window. Capture 92.2 percent non-black, bbox (14,12)-(654,759), rows reading row N revision 1"
2026-09-16,windows,winui,P57,ok,ok,window,default,"#117 lazy rows on WinUIBackend, and the defect the memory measurement could not see. The saving was real from bde16de0 (10000 rows 143 MB against 328 eager, with a control) and the rows NEVER RENDERED. Four arrangements: content set in phase 0 draws the placeholder index 0 1 2 because ListViewBase preparation runs after the handler; args.handled=true draws nothing while a probe reads content=Canvas back so the assignment worked; registerUpdateCallback in a later phase draws nothing and the placeholder numbers vanish so it does fire; clearing container.contentTemplate first RENDERS. Cause: a container WinUI GENERATES for a data item carries a ContentTemplate to render that item, and a UIElement assigned as Content is not shown directly while a template is in place. The eager path never hit it because it builds its own ListViewItems. Re-measured rendering: 1 row 130 MB, 400 139, 10000 146 against eager 143/328. Pointer replay P57-lazy-list-winui.csv 20 actions: selection=1 then 9 at the same y after scrolling, revision=1, rows=1 then 10000, selection=9999. Capture 96.0 percent non-black, rows reading row N revision 1"
2026-09-16,windows,gtk4,P20,ok,ok,n/a,default,"#121 keyboard shortcut and #79 window height, same run. Ctrl+K replay of P20-ctrl-k-shortcut.csv: 5 actions replayed, SHORTCUT FIRED exactly once - not zero (accelerator never reached the item) and not twice (the began=5 shape). #79: the decoration IS measurable before present. Three existing probes all report 47 because all three measure a GtkHeaderBar this code built; GTK own decoration is a different instance at 39 and gtk_window_get_titlebar cannot reach it because that getter answers only for a CUSTOM titlebar. Walking a realized never-presented window children finds it: gtkOwn=39 before present. Shortfall went 0x-9 to 0x0 on P57 (no menu bar) and 0x-1 on P20 (menu bar). The comment blaming realization was measured false - the insideAWindow branch realizes and still reports 47"
2026-09-16,windows,winui,P20,ok,ok,n/a,default,"#121 keyboard shortcut on WinUIBackend: 5 actions replayed, SHORTCUT FIRED exactly once. Needed a harness fix first - the replay refused every key-carrying file whenever another application held the foreground, reporting AttachThreadInput failed (87). 87 is ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER, not the ACCESS_DENIED the code anticipated: the foreground was a ConsoleWindowClass window belonging to PowerToys.Awake.exe and a console host thread cannot be attached to. takeForegroundByAttachingInput was judging the MECHANISM not the OUTCOME; it now asks for the foreground anyway and reads GetForegroundWindow back. WinUI took the foreground WITHOUT attaching, gtk4 attached to Progman"
2026-09-16,windows,winui,P38,ok,n/a,n/a,default,"#P38 WebView2 still dead, and the recorded fix is REFUTED. Reproduced: CoInitializeEx(STA) returns 0x80010106 RPC_E_CHANGED_MODE and no completion ever follows, so EnsureCoreWebView2Async never reports finished. The source comment said the fix was one word upstream, .multi to .single in swift-winui SwiftApplication.swift. Made to actually run this time - checkout edited AND the WinUI module build products deleted so it could not be skipped, POSITIVE CONTROL Compiling WinUI = 2 hits against the 0 that made the 2026-09-10 attempt meaningless. The probe still returned 0x80010106. The different-thread escape was closed too: WinUIApplication.main() logs thread 18076 and beginEnsureCore logs 18076, same thread. So RoInitialize(RO_INIT_SINGLETHREADED) succeeded on this thread and it was still not STA. Something between RoInitialize and the WebView puts the thread in MTA - Application.start, the WindowsAppRuntime bootstrapper, or the XAML dispatcher setup. Checkout restored to .multi and made read-only again"
2026-09-11,android,android,P57,n/a,n/a,n/a,unrecorded,"Android now COMPILES here. Every android build had been failing with -module compiled with Swift 6.3.3 cannot be imported by the Swift 6.4 compiler-, whose first error named a stale SwiftSyntax.swiftmodule in the build directory - a symptom. The cause was the host toolchain being 6.4 while the installed Android SDK is swift-6.3.3-RELEASE, and a matching toolchain was installed all along. compile.zsh now matches them itself. P57, P64, P65, P67 and P68 all build for aarch64-unknown-linux-android31 with zero errors. Compiling is not running: none of it has been on a device."
