2026-09-15,android,android,P57,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#117 phase 3 on Android: dumpsys meminfo TOTAL PSS went from 385 MB to 92 MB at 10,000 rows, and 1 row is 91 MB - flat, not merely smaller. The provider was called fewer than 500 times for 10,000 rows. The earlier Android runs in this session measured a STALE APK: compile.zsh -android builds into .build/ while the APK is packaged from a separate swift-bundler build, so the compile returned 0, adb install printed Success, and the screenshots were of the previous build. P57 now prints `lazy rows: yes/NO` from `backend is any BackendFeatures.LazyListRows` plus a control line for ScrollingLists, which is what identified the stale binary; recorded as mistakes.md entry 8."
2026-09-16,mac,appkit,P69,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#123 accessibility modifiers. ax_dump (external, reads the real AX tree) on P69: AXButton desc='Close' on a button showing 'X'; desc='Delete' help='Removes the file permanently'; desc='Volume' value='40 percent'; AXStaticText 'decorative' absent. Three earlier attempts failed silently and are recorded in the source: setting the label in commit (updateButton rewrites it from computeLayout on the next pass), finding the inner control by isAccessibilityElement() (false by default on every NSView), and by unique NSControl descendant (there are two). The override is now held by NSCustomButton and consulted by its own derivation. Also fixed: every button was announced twice - setAccessibilityElement(false) does not remove an NSTextField from the tree, setAccessibilityChildren([button]) does."
2026-09-16,android,android,P69,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#123 accessibility modifiers. uiautomator dump --compressed on P69: Button desc='Close', Button desc='Delete' hint='Removes the file permanently', Button desc='Volume'; TextViews 'X', 'Delete', 'Volume' and 'decorative' all absent. View.setTooltipText was tried for the hint and left the node's hint attribute EMPTY - AccessibilityNodeInfo.hintText needs a delegate, so SwiftAccessibilityDelegate.kt carries hint and stateDescription. The PLAIN uiautomator dump is the wrong probe for accessibilityHidden: UiAutomation sets FLAG_INCLUDE_NOT_IMPORTANT_VIEWS, so it showed 'decorative' present while the flag was correctly set. stateDescription is not in uiautomator's attribute set; it rides the same delegate override as the hint, which is verified."
2026-09-16,ios,uikit,P69,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#123 accessibility modifiers. The app's own read-back is on screen (iOS has no external AX vantage): 'Close'/h''/v'' | 'Delete'/h'Removes the file permanently'/v'' | 'Volume'/h''/v'40 percent' | HIDDEN - the HIDDEN marker is the walk refusing to descend into accessibilityElementsHidden, and nothing follows it. namedChild walks for UIControl or UILabel rather than isAccessibilityElement, which is false by default on UIView."
2026-09-16,mac,appkit,P70,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#122 focus. Driven by real CGEvent clicks (actions/mac/P70-focus.csv), not by the app: changes=1 from AppKit's own initial first responder, 2 and 3 from clicks on the two fields, 'focused field' agreeing with the focus ring, refusal check 'refused (correct)'. Two defects found and fixed: rebuilding the focus observer each frame swallowed changes silently (it runs from computeLayout, so a fresh observer compares a change against itself); and a .disabled(true) button took the keyboard, because the responder search walks past the plain-NSView wrapper to the inner enabled NSButton."
2026-09-16,android,android,P70,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#122 focus. Driven by adb input tap: focused field email, changes 2, then 3 when the tap moved off it; refusal check 'refused (correct)'. View.setOnFocusChangeListener reported NOTHING first -- a SwiftCrossUI widget is a container and that listener fires only for the view it is set on; a tap put a caret in the email field while the app showed 'focused field: none' and 'changes: 0'. Fixed with ViewTreeObserver.OnGlobalFocusChangeListener, the counterpart of watching AppKit's window first responder. Focus indicator measured rather than assumed: the focused field's underline goes 3px (73,69,79) to 5px (98,91,113) -- Material's thicker accent underline, not macOS's blue ring."
2026-09-16,ios,uikit,P70,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#122 focus. focused field email, changes 2, refusal check 'refused (correct)', caret visible in the field. THE MOVES ARE APP-DRIVEN, under SCUI_P70_AUTOFOCUS (passed as SIMCTL_CHILD_SCUI_P70_AUTOFOCUS), because testapp/actions/ios is empty and planned -- so the user-caused half is NOT covered on iOS and the app says so in its own comment. What it does prove is the notification path: the counter rises only if UITextField's begin/end editing notification came back, so a handler that never fired would show as a caret beside a zero."
2026-09-16,android,android,P70,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"AndroidBackend on Swift 6 language mode, run on the emulator. Four launch flags arrive intact (CommandLine.arguments echoed back all four words the launching intent carried, a flag and two value-carrying options among them), zero crashes, process alive, focus changes heard 3, refusal check 'refused (correct)'. The setter CommandLine.arguments needs was obsoleted in Swift 6.0 and is reached by symbol -- verified present with nm -D libswiftCore.so on the SDK in use, with __owned spelled out because a property setter takes its value owned and getting that wrong is a leak or a crash rather than a build error."
2026-09-16,mac,appkit,P71,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#121 step 2, menu keyboard shortcuts. Driven by real CGEvent key presses with the MENU CLOSED the whole time (actions/mac/P71-shortcuts.csv): Cmd-S fired the plain item once, Cmd-Shift-E fired the shifted item once, and Cmd-D did NOT fire the disabled item -- plain 1, shifted 1, disabled 0 on screen. The menu is never opened on purpose: opening it and clicking the item tests the item, which already worked, while the whole of #121 is the half where the key works without the menu. A backend that draws the glyph and never wires the action leaves the first two at zero. AppKit lowercases the key equivalent and carries Shift in the modifier mask only, because an uppercase keyEquivalent already means Shift to AppKit and the mask would then ask for it twice."
2026-09-16,windows,winui,P70,ok,ok,n/a,default,"#122 focus driven by P70-focus.csv, 13 actions. All four protocol methods pass: focus() moves it (FOCUS now name/email), setFocusChangeHandler fires (changes 2..7, a counter stuck at 0 is the failure this app exists to catch), unfocus() returns it to none, and the disabled button is REFUSED. The refusal reads as true-then-false in the log and both lines are correct: FocusState does if !backend.focus(widget) { binding.wrappedValue = false }, so the app sets the binding optimistically and the backend refusal writes it back. The verdict is the LAST value, or the line the app renders (disabled button reports focused: no (correct)). Reading the first line as a failure cost a round and is mistakes.md entry 14"
2026-09-16,windows,gtk4,P70,ok,ok,n/a,default,"#122 focus driven by P70-focus-gtk4.csv, 13 actions, same assertions as the WinUI half at this backend own coordinates (window 588x628 against WinUI 562x604). changes 1..6, name/email/none in order, disabled button refused. GtkEventControllerFocus is hand-written: zero hits in Sources/Gtk/Generated and none needed, because Sources/CGtk/header.h is the whole gtk.h. enter/leave on the controller rather than notify::has-focus on the widget, because a TextField here is a wrapper around a GtkEntry and the wrapper own has-focus stays false the whole time a user types into it"
2026-09-16,windows,winui,P69,ok,n/a,n/a,default,"#123 accessibility, and the first Windows verification of it. P69 readback lived entirely inside #if canImport(UIKit); the #else branch printed ACCESSIBILITY READ having read nothing, so a Windows run looked like a pass and asserted zero. Added an in-process readback through an embedded WinUIElementRepresentable, since there is no global window list. All four modifiers land on the right element and only their own field: label=Close, hint=Removes the file permanently, value=40 percent, hidden=true. LIMIT: in-process, so it confirms the backend attached what the modifier asked for and says nothing about what Narrator resolves. GTK gets NO readback at all - gtkaccessible.h has update_property/update_state and no getters, so the properties are write-only in-process and reading needs AT-SPI, which is Linux-only"
2026-09-16,mac,appkit,P71,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#121 step 2, menu keyboard shortcuts. Driven by real CGEvent key presses with the MENU CLOSED the whole time (actions/mac/P71-shortcuts.csv): Cmd-S fired the plain item once, Cmd-Shift-E fired the shifted item once, and Cmd-D did NOT fire the disabled item -- plain 1, shifted 1, disabled 0 on screen. The menu is never opened on purpose: opening it and clicking the item tests the item, which already worked, while the whole of #121 is the half where the key works without the menu. A backend that draws the glyph and never wires the action leaves the first two at zero. AppKit lowercases the key equivalent and carries Shift in the modifier mask only, because an uppercase keyEquivalent already means Shift to AppKit and the mask would then ask for it twice."
2026-09-16,ios,uikit,P71,ok,blocked,window,unrecorded,"#121 on UIKit: BUILT, NOT DRIVEN, and the blocker was measured rather than assumed. UIKeyCommand carries a key and takes a SELECTOR dispatched through the responder chain, so the closure lives in MenuShortcutActions behind a token in propertyList and the selector is on ApplicationDelegate -- a UIResponder AND the object that builds the menu. Driving it failed: on an iPhone simulator buildMenu is never called with .main so no key command is ever registered, and on an iPad (iPad Pro 13-inch M5, iOS 27.0) all three counters stayed 0. A POSITIVE CONTROL settled what that zero meant: P70 under SCUI_P70_AUTOFOCUS showed 'focused field: email' with a caret, and three plain letters sent the same way did NOT appear in the field -- so keystrokes do not reach the app at all through CGEvent into DeviceHub, and the zero says nothing about the implementation. No simctl sendkey verb exists, idb is not installed, and DeviceHub exposes no menu bar over accessibility."
2026-09-16,android,android,P71,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#121 on Android: CANNOT PASS YET, and the reason is upstream of the shortcut. All three counters stayed 0 after input keycombination sent CTRL+S, CTRL+SHIFT+E and CTRL+D with the menu closed. The cause is not the shortcut code: AndroidBackend.setApplicationMenu is COMMENTED OUT in AndroidBackend.swift:544 with upstream's own TODO 'Register app menu items as shortcuts when we support keyboard shortcuts', so .commands and CommandMenu produce nothing on Android -- there is no application menu for a shortcut to live in. The setAlphabeticShortcut call added today sits in the AttachedMenus path, which builds a PopupMenu for in-view Menu views, and a PopupMenu dispatches no keyboard shortcut while closed. So Android's real gap is the application menu itself, which is a larger CLAUDE.md debt than the shortcut; #121 on Android is blocked behind it. input keycombination is available on this emulator and works, so the driving path is not the obstacle."
2026-09-16,windows,winui,P57,ok,probe,n/a,unrecorded,"#117 row lifetimes on WinUI, verified against a control. WinUIBackend conformed to LazyListRows but not LazyListRowLifetimes, so no row was ever reported released and List.swift had already switched this backend to the 4000-row backstop -- worse than not conforming. Released now on ContainerContentChanging + inRecycleQueue with args.itemIndex. One binary, two configurations interleaved twice, each sweeping 5000 rows through the new in-process --winui-list-probe (no mouse, no action file): releasing 146 and 150 MB, SCUI_WINUI_NO_LAZY_RELEASE=1 control 221 and 222 MB, no overlap; ~9000 prepares and ~9000 recycles and 38 realized containers in all four, so the 72-76 MB is framework row nodes. Three plausible designs measured false first: ObjectIdentifier is not a container identity (one address served rows 2-7 then 14-18, per-access COM wrappers), Tag does not round-trip (LOST 95 of 95), and no row was ever re-prepared while still live so no rebind path is needed. Regenerate: zsh matrix_coverage/coverage.zsh"
2026-09-16,ios,uikit,P71,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#121 on UIKit: DRIVEN AND PASSING on an iPad (iPad Pro 13-inch M5, iOS 27.0) through the supported path, testapp/actions/ios/P71-shortcuts.csv replayed by test_ios.zsh --actionfile. plain 1, shifted 1, disabled 0, with the menu closed throughout. The root cause was not the shortcut: UIKitBackend's ApplicationMenus conformance was behind #if targetEnvironment(macCatalyst), so on real iOS the framework's 'backend as? any BackendFeatures.ApplicationMenus' failed, setApplicationMenu was never called, and buildMenu ran against an empty submenu list -- traced by having buildMenu write what it saw: 'buildMenu system==main: true submenus=0'. So .commands and CommandMenu did nothing at all on iOS, not just their shortcuts. The #if carried upstream's own condition, 'Once keyboard shortcuts are implemented, it might be possible to do them on more platforms than just Mac Catalyst', which today's work met. Also added: keydown/keyup/key in the XCUITest runner, which threw unsupported for all three."
2026-09-16,windows,winui,P23,ok,probe,window,unrecorded,"#125 table row selection, the framework-to-backend half. New protocol BackendFeatures.TableSelection (setSelectionHandler + setSelectedRow) with Table(rows, selection:) on the view side. WinUITable is a Grid with no row object, so the highlight is a Border inserted at children index 0 (behind the cells) spanning the row. Driven with the new mouse-free --select-probe, which writes the binding on a timer: row selection supported: yes, SELECTION now 2 / 5 / none. PICTURES, because the log alone cannot see a highlight that was never drawn -- that is exactly how #117 WinUI passed on memory while rendering nothing: p23-sel-row5-20260916-134321.png shows the band on the ID=3 row (index 2, zero-based, matching the readout) with the text still legible over it, and p23-sel-none-20260916-134324.png shows it gone. The OTHER direction, a click becoming a binding write, is not covered by THIS run; it was driven and passed later the same day (see the P23 winui row with replay=ok), and the claim that mouse injection is refused while a remote-desktop host is connected turned out to be false."
2026-09-16,windows,gtk4,P23,ok,probe,window,unrecorded,"#125 table row selection, the framework-to-backend half, GtkBackend. Gtk.Table is a GtkGrid inside a ScrolledWindow with no row object either, so the highlight is a CSS class added to that row cells, with a display-wide CSSProvider carrying a literal rgba (a theme name the running theme does not define is a silent CSS parse error, and a dropped rule reads exactly like selection not working). Same --select-probe: row selection supported: yes, SELECTION now 2 / 5 / none. p23gtk-sel-row5-20260916-134434.png shows the band on the ID=6 row -- index 5, a DIFFERENT row from the WinUI capture, so the highlight follows the binding rather than being painted in a fixed place -- and p23gtk-sel-none-20260916-134436.png shows it cleared. Click-to-binding NOT verified in THIS run; it was driven and passed later the same day, see the P23 gtk4 row recorded with replay=ok."
2026-09-16,android,android,P71,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#121 and the application menu on Android: plain 1, shifted 1, disabled 0, driven by 'adb shell input keycombination' with the menu never opened. setApplicationMenu was a commented-out stub carrying upstream's TODO 'Register app menu items as shortcuts when we support keyboard shortcuts'; that is now what it does. Android has no application menu to draw -- an app's global actions belong to a toolbar overflow or a drawer, which are the app's own layout, and drawing one here would add a bar to every SwiftCrossUI app on Android whether it declared .commands or not. What survives the translation is the shortcut. Uses View.addOnUnhandledKeyEventListener rather than setOnKeyListener: the unhandled variant runs only after every view has declined the event, so Cmd-S does not get swallowed before a focused text field sees it and a plain 's' typed into that field never arrives. Modifier comparison is exact rather than subset, or Ctrl-Shift-S would also fire a plain Ctrl-S entry; CAPS_LOCK and NUM_LOCK are masked out, or every shortcut would stop working with Caps Lock on."
2026-09-16,mac,appkit,P23,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#125 table row selection on AppKitBackend, BOTH DIRECTIONS, driven by testapp/actions/mac/P23-row-selection.csv. The click half -- which the two Windows rows above could not verify because mouse injection is refused there -- is what the log line SELECTION now 4 proves: nothing else in P23 produces a 4, so that line can only have come from the click on row 5. Then Clear selection -> none, then Select row 2 -> 2, and p23-macos-final-20260916-135911.png carries the band on the ID=3 row with the readout reading 2. The table and the selectable list are the same NSCustomTableView here, so the click path already existed and createTable had simply left allowSelections off; it is turned on when a handler arrives, not in createTable, so a table written without selection: stays unselectable rather than highlighting under the pointer and reporting to nothing. One real fix on the way: selectionIndexesForProposedSelection force-unwrapped proposedSelectionIndexes.first, which is safe for a selectable list (never empty) and a crash the first time a table user clicks below the last row."
2026-09-16,ios,uikit,P23,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#125 table row selection on UIKitBackend, BOTH DIRECTIONS, two action files because the verdict is on the screen: P23Diagnostics writes to a log in the working directory and inside a simulator container that write does not land, and the simulator log carries no app stdout -- measured, no such file under CoreSimulator/Devices after a full run. testapp/actions/ios/P23-row-selection.csv ends on Select row 2 and p23-ios-final-20260916-140223.png shows the band on the ID=3 row with the readout at 2; testapp/actions/ios/P23-tap-a-row.csv selects row 2 FIRST and then taps row 5, so the band has to MOVE -- p23-ios-final-20260916-140114.png shows it on the ID=5 row with the readout at 4, which a single tap on an already-highlighted row could not have proved. None of this came free: this table is not a UITableView (TableWidget lays its cells out in layoutSubviews because the width is unknown when the cells arrive), so selection is a highlight view sent to the back on every pass plus a tap recogniser, installed once while the handler is replaced on every commit."
2026-09-16,android,android,P23,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#125 table row selection on AndroidBackend, BOTH DIRECTIONS, two action files for the same reason as iOS: no p23-debug-events.log comes back from the device and the harness log carries no [P23] line, so the verdict is read off the capture. testapp/actions/android/P23-row-selection.csv -> p23-android-final-20260916-140651.png, band on the ID=3 row, readout 2; testapp/actions/android/P23-tap-a-row.csv selects row 2 first then taps the ID=2 row so the band must move -> p23-android-final-20260916-140804.png, band on ID=2, readout 1. The highlight is DRAWN in TableContainer.onDraw rather than made a child view, the opposite of UIKit and for a platform reason: a ViewGroup draws itself before its children, so the rectangle is already behind every cell with no ordering to maintain -- but setWillNotDraw(false) is required or onDraw is skipped entirely and the failure is silent, right colour, right rectangle, nothing on screen. onTouchEvent must consume ACTION_DOWN or ACTION_UP never arrives. Coordinates come from a uiautomator dump, not the capture: row 3 wraps to nine lines so the rows are not evenly spaced and a row picked by eye lands on the wrong one."
2026-09-16,windows,winui,P23,ok,ok,n/a,unrecorded,"#125 the OTHER half: a click becomes a binding write, driven with actions/win/P23-select-rows.csv and no probe involved. Three clicks, and the middle one is the control: sixth row -> SELECTION now 5 (zero-based, so a backend reporting the ID or the on-screen number would say 6), HEADER -> nothing at all, first row -> SELECTION now 0. Instrumented with SCUI_WINUI_TABLE_TRACE: pointer handler attached, pointerPressed fired three times, handleClick y=172/10/32 against heights [18, 28, 28, 28] matching definition 6/0/1. The header being 18 px and the rows 28 is why the hit test accumulates RowDefinition.actualHeight instead of dividing by a row height -- dividing would have put all three clicks one row out. Mouse injection worked with Chrome Remote Desktop connected, contradicting the blocker warning that had been treated as settled: the earlier failure to see any selection came from launching through the harness, not from input being refused. Regenerate: zsh matrix_coverage/coverage.zsh"
2026-09-16,windows,gtk4,P23,ok,ok,n/a,unrecorded,"#125 click-to-binding on GtkBackend, actions/win/P23-select-rows-gtk4.csv -- a separate file because the two windows differ in size and the rows are not in the same places (848x688 from y=368 against WinUI 822x652 from y=325); sharing coordinates would click the header and still report success. Identical verdict through a completely different path (gtk_widget_pick, walk up to the grid direct child, gtk_grid_query_child): sixth row -> SELECTION now 5, HEADER -> nothing, first row -> SELECTION now 0. Two backends, two hit-test mechanisms, three clicks each, same three answers."
2026-09-16,windows,gtk4,P57,ok,ok,n/a,unrecorded,"#117 GTK lazy rows driven with a real pointer at last -- actions/win/P57-lazy-list-gtk4.csv, 20 of 20 actions replayed. selection=1 on the first click at y=532; after six wheel notches the SAME y gives selection=8, which is the assertion this file exists for: clicking one point and getting a different row is what says the list scrolled rather than merely redrew. Update rows -> revision=1, Clear -> selection=none, Toggle count -> rows=1 then rows=400, and Select last on the rebuilt 400-row list reports 399 -- so the lazy path still addresses rows correctly after a wholesale rebuild rather than holding stale indices. Coordinates re-measured a third time first (p57gtk-layout-20260916-185143.png): the row release reported: line added for #117 lifetimes pushed the list down 26 px, so the old y=506 written as row 1 had become row 0. Four earlier attempts between 18:52 and 18:57 were refused with SetCursorPos status 0 and the cursor frozen at (266,394); a control replay of the P23 file that had passed at 13:52 failed in the same minutes, so it was the machine and not this app. It worked on the next attempt with no change to the file."
2026-09-16,windows,winui,P23,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#125 column sorting on WinUI, driven and passing. New protocol BackendFeatures.TableColumnSorting (setSortHandler + setSortIndicator), TableSortOrder (a column index and a direction, NOT a comparator: a TableColumn here is a closure with no key path, so the app sorts and the framework only reports). actions/win/P23-sort-columns.csv, 9 actions: button -> column 3 ascending firstId=1 lastId=8; the same button again -> column 3 DESCENDING firstId=8 lastId=1, which is the flip and also says the ROWS moved rather than only the binding; the Number HEADER itself -> column 3 ascending, through pointerPressed and the columnIndex(atX:) hit test that accumulates ColumnDefinition.actualWidth -- a backend reporting the wrong column would read as sorted by ID and still look right; Clear sort -> none, ids 1/8. The direction flip lives in Table.commit rather than in each backend, so five backends cannot drift on it."
2026-09-16,windows,gtk4,P23,ok,blocked,window,unrecorded,"#125 column sorting on GtkBackend: BUILT AND NOT DRIVEN, recorded separately from the WinUI row on purpose. The implementation reads the column out of gtk_grid_query_child, which returns column and row together, so a header click is (column, 0) with no x arithmetic -- a genuinely different mechanism from WinUI actualWidth accumulation, which is why driving it is a real test and not the same test twice. actions/win/P23-sort-columns-gtk4.csv exists with coordinates measured off p23gtk-sort-layout-20260916-191500.png (848x688; the Number header at x=790 against WinUI 632, so nothing transfers). Two attempts at 19:15 and 19:16 were refused: SetCursorPos status 0, cursor frozen at (260,386) which is where the WinUI sort run left it at 19:10. Same intermittency as the P57 replay earlier, which succeeded on its fifth attempt with nothing changed."
2026-09-16,windows,gtk4,P23,ok,ok,n/a,unrecorded,"#125 column sorting on GtkBackend: DRIVEN AND PASSING, superseding the blocked row recorded minutes earlier. actions/win/P23-sort-columns-gtk4.csv gives the same four answers as WinUI through a different mechanism (gtk_grid_query_child returns column and row together, so a header is (column, 0) with no x arithmetic): column 3 ascending firstId=1 lastId=8; the same button again column 3 DESCENDING firstId=8 lastId=1; the Number HEADER itself column 3 ascending via the backend; Clear sort none. Two backends, two hit tests, identical verdicts. NOT captured: a picture of the sort INDICATOR. actions/win/P23-sort-indicator-gtk4.csv exists for exactly that -- one click then a six-second hold -- and eight consecutive attempts were refused with SetCursorPos status 0. So the arrow is implemented (setSortIndicator appends U+25B2/U+25BC to the header text and is reapplied after setColumnLabels rebuilds the labels) and has NOT been seen on screen. The log cannot stand in for that: column 3 ascending reads identically whether the indicator drew or drew nothing, which is the #117 WinUI shape. Correlation worth recording rather than a cause: both sort runs that succeeded today began immediately after the user interacted with the remote session, and the eight that failed ran with no interaction at all."
2026-09-16,windows,gtk4,P23,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#125 the sort INDICATOR, seen at last: p23gtk-indicator-20260916-193639.png shows Number with a triangle after it while ID, the long header and Short carry none -- the control is in the same picture, so it says the arrow goes on the SORTED column rather than on every header. The readout beside it says sort: column 3 ascending and the log agrees. This is the piece the log could never supply: column 3 ascending reads identically whether setSortIndicator drew something or nothing, the #117 WinUI shape. Driven with actions/win/P23-sort-indicator-gtk4.csv, which clicks once and holds six seconds so the capture lands while the sort is live; the four-step file ends with Clear sort and its capture is necessarily arrow-free. THIRD CONFIRMATION of the input pattern: eight consecutive attempts minutes earlier were refused with no user interaction, this one ran immediately after the user interacted with the remote session and was refused zero times."
2026-09-16,windows,winui,P23,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#125 the sort INDICATOR on WinUI, the twin of the GtkBackend capture: p23win-indicator-20260916-193911.png shows Number with a triangle while ID, the long header and Short carry none. The three unmarked headers are the control in the same picture -- an implementation that appended the arrow to every header, and one that rebuilt the labels and lost it, both produce pictures the log cannot tell apart from this one. Driven with actions/win/P23-sort-indicator.csv (one click, six-second hold) because P23-sort-columns.csv ends with Clear sort and its capture is necessarily arrow-free. Both Windows backends now have a picture for every part of #125: selection in both directions, the four sort transitions, and the indicator. FOURTH confirmation of the input pattern: refused zero times, run immediately after the user said they had moved the mouse."
2026-09-16,windows,gtk4,P70,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#122 focus on GtkBackend, re-driven after 9cb8d81e reverted the change that had been made against a misread log, and passing. All four protocol methods exercised: FOCUS now name/email/name/none/email/none with changes 1..6 (focus, unfocus and the change handler), then the refusal. THE VERDICT IS THE LAST VALUE AND THE RENDERED LINE, which is the thing that cost a round this morning: the log shows DISABLED FOCUS reported true and then false in the same second, and the false is the refusal WORKING -- @FocusState sets the binding optimistically and the framework writes it back with if !backend.focus(widget) { binding.wrappedValue = false }. The screenshot p70gtk-focus-20260916-194048.png renders the answer directly: focus changes heard: 6, refusal check: refused (correct), disabled button reports focused: no (correct), plain Text reports focused: no. See mistakes.md entry 14. Refused zero times; run while the mouse was live."
2026-09-16,mac,appkit,P23,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#125 sortOrder on AppKitBackend, both directions, testapp/actions/mac/P23-column-sorting.csv. Three clicks and the middle one is the point: the Number header, the SAME header again, then the Sort by Number BUTTON. Log: SORT now column 3 ascending firstId=1 lastId=8 / column 3 descending firstId=8 lastId=1 / column 3 ascending firstId=1 lastId=8. The second line is what one click cannot test -- a backend that reported the column but remembered nothing would restart at ascending with its first click, its arrow and its binding all correct. AppKit gets sortable headers from NSTableColumn.sortDescriptorPrototype, set on every commit because setColumnLabels rebuilds every column; a prototype set once attaches to columns that are then thrown away and the header stops responding after the first data change. One defect found by the action file and not by the compiler: removing a column drops its sort descriptor, NSTableView announces that as a sort change, and the binding oscillated ascending/none/ascending/none forever -- a screenshot of either end of that loop is a screenshot of a table working. Fixed by widening the guard from setSortOrder to any framework-driven mutation."
2026-09-16,ios,uikit,P23,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#125 sortOrder on UIKitBackend, three action files because iOS returns no log: P23-column-sorting.csv (two taps on one header -> column 3 descending, first/last 8/1, triangle down), P23-sort-two-columns.csv (column 3 then column 0 -> column 0 ascending, which pins that a DIFFERENT column starts ascending rather than inheriting), P23-sort-from-code.csv (the button only -> column 3 ascending with the header never touched, so only setSortIndicator can have drawn the arrow). Nothing came from UIKit: this table is not a UITableView, so the arrow is a UILabel moved between header cells and the hit test is the same tap recogniser the selection uses, hoisted out of setSelectionHandler because a table given sortOrder and no selection would otherwise have had no recogniser at all. Also fixed in P23 itself: the sort readout sat beside its buttons, and 'sort: column 3 ascending' wraps to two lines on a phone, which moved the table down and made the second tap land above the header -- the capture showed a correct arrow and read as a backend that had forgotten its sort order."
2026-09-16,android,android,P23,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#125 sortOrder on AndroidBackend, three action files for the same reason as iOS: P23-column-sorting.csv (column 3 descending), P23-sort-two-columns.csv (column 0 ascending), P23-sort-from-code.csv (column 3 ascending from the button). The triangle is drawn with a Path in TableContainer.onDraw rather than added to the header's text, because setColumnLabels rebuilds every header view from Swift on every commit and an arrow baked into a string would be erased by the next update; its colour is the header's own currentTextColor, since a hard-coded black disappears on a dark theme and reads as an indicator that never drew. Coordinates come from a uiautomator dump rather than the capture. Build note: swift-bundler builds through a SEPARATE scratch path (.build-bundler) whose release.yaml went stale and omitted a newly added source file, so the error pointed at the files USING TableSortOrder rather than at the one declaring it; .build/release.yaml had it and .build-bundler/release.yaml was four hours old."
2026-09-16,windows,winui,P50,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#109 popover arrowEdge on WinUI, driven and passing with a PAIR of captures, which is the only form this evidence can take. New protocol BackendFeatures.PopoverArrowEdges (setPreferredArrowEdge) plus .popover(isPresented:arrowEdge:onDismiss:content:); separate protocol rather than a parameter on presentPopover, which would have broken all five conformances at once and four of them cannot be compiled here (mistakes entry 10). WinUI stores the preference on the Popover and presentPopover reads it, because that method assigns placement immediately before showAt and would otherwise overwrite it -- the preference would take effect on the NEXT presentation, wrong once then right forever, which reads as a race. p50-alpha-top-20260916-202313.png shows PANEL ALPHA ABOVE its button (readout: arrow edge: top) and p50-alpha-bottom-20260916-202205.png shows it BELOW the same button (readout: bottom). TWO EARLIER ARRANGEMENTS WERE DISCARDED AS UNFALSIFIABLE and the action file records both: the second button has no room below or to its left, so .bottom flipped above and .leading flipped right, each producing a capture identical to its opposite. A pair that cannot differ proves nothing whichever way the code behaves."
2026-09-16,windows,gtk4,P50,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#109 popover arrowEdge on GtkBackend, driven through GtkPopover.position -- a different API from WinUI FlyoutPlacementMode, so the two runs are two tests rather than one done twice. actions/win/P50-arrow-edge-gtk4.csv, a separate file because this window captures 808x1005 against WinUI 782x918 and the cycle button is at y=756 here and y=702 there. Both transitions landed with zero refusals: ARROW EDGE now top then popover alpha shown, ARROW EDGE now bottom then popover alpha shown. PICTURE FOR .top ONLY, and recorded that way rather than covered by the WinUI pair: p50gtk-edge-top-20260916-203851.png caught the popover WINDOW and shows its beak pointing DOWN, which is the panel sitting above its anchor -- stronger evidence than a relative position because the popover draws the direction itself. The .bottom twin is missing for a tooling reason, not a behavioural one: a GTK popover is its own top-level window sharing the title, so a window capture races between the two, and a desktop capture makes the popover light-dismiss on focus loss (two desktop captures show the main window with the readout and no panel). A later attempt to catch it was refused by the mouse."
2026-09-16,windows,gtk4,P50,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#109 the GtkBackend pair is COMPLETE, closing the gap recorded an hour earlier. p50gtk-edge-top-20260916-203851.png shows the panel with its beak pointing DOWN (above its anchor) and p50gtk-beakdown-20260916-204601.png shows it pointing UP (below the same anchor). Stronger than the WinUI pair in one respect: a GtkPopover DRAWS the direction, so the picture states it rather than leaving it to be inferred from where the panel sits. Getting the second one needed actions/win/P50-arrow-edge-bottom-gtk4.csv, a one-preference file, and the reason is capture tooling rather than behaviour: the popover is its own top-level window sharing the main window title, so screenshot.zsh -w P50 picks between them and picked the main window on three attempts, while a desktop capture loses the popover to light dismiss. The one capture that had landed on the popover came during a run FIRST hold, so this file makes .bottom the first hold. Zero refusals; run immediately after the user said the mouse was enabled, which is now the fifth time that has worked and the pattern this machine is driven by."
2026-09-16,windows,winui,P42,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#80 display-scale notification on WinUI: PASSES, driven by a human changing Settings > System > Display > Scale from 100% to 125% with the window untouched. Log: scale factor -> 1.0 (change 1) at 12:51:28, then scale factor -> 1.25 (change 2) at 12:53:18, with nothing in between. Screen p42win-after125-20260916-205403.png reads current: 1.25, changes observed: 1, and the history 1.0 x5 -> 1.25 x2. THE HISTORY IS THE ASSERTION, not the current value: 1.25 alone would also be true of an app launched after the change, so only a window that saw 1.0 FIRST and 1.25 second proves the notification fired. Not touching the window is load-bearing -- clicking or resizing forces a layout pass that would update the value even if no notification arrived, which makes a broken feature look correct. This needs a person because the change is a system broadcast from the Settings UI; writing the registry produces no broadcast, so a scripted version would measure an event of its own making."
2026-09-16,windows,gtk4,P42,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#80 display-scale notification on GtkBackend: the window never saw a change (changes observed: 0, history 1.0 x7) after the user moved the display from 100% to 125% without touching it -- AND THAT IS NOT THE DEFECT IT LOOKS LIKE. The control settles it: killed and relaunched WHILE the display was at 125%, a fresh window still reports 1.0. So GDK on Windows is not expressing the display scale through the surface scale factor at all; it reports 1 at both settings and scales through font DPI instead. Subscribing to notify::scale-factor would therefore change nothing, because the value never moves. Without that control the two explanations are indistinguishable on screen -- both show current: 1.0 and changes observed: 0 -- and the wrong one leads to implementing a notification for a change that does not exist. WinUI passed the same test in the same minutes (1.0 -> 1.25, change 2), so this is a GDK-on-Windows fact rather than anything about the framework. OPEN QUESTION for whoever takes this next: what does GTK on Windows expose instead -- gdk_surface_get_scale (fractional, GTK 4.12+), or only the font DPI -- and is windowScaleFactor even the right channel for it here."
2026-09-16,windows,gtk4,P42,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#80 on GtkBackend, CORRECTING row 834 of the same day. That row concluded that GDK on Windows is not expressing the display scale at all, and stopped there; the user answered -- I think that it is reasonable for GTK to follow the scale factor on windows -- and following that found the conclusion half right and the stopping point wrong. WHAT ROW 834 GOT RIGHT, now confirmed with the fractional API it did not have: at 125%, gtk_widget_get_scale_factor=1, gdk_surface_get_scale=1.0 (the GTK 4.12 double; GTK here is 4.22, so it exists and it answered 1.0), gdk_surface_get_scale_factor=1. No GDK call states the display scale. The display really was at 125%: P42-WinUI on the same desktop minutes apart logged scale factor -> 1.25. WHAT ROW 834 GOT WRONG is calling that a platform fact. Win32 GetDpiForWindow answers 1.25 on the same window in the same instant, reached through gdk_win32_surface_get_handle -- the value is available, GDK just does not carry it. Implemented as scui_window_display_scale() in Sources/GtkCHelpers/gtk_window_scale.c, read by computeWindowEnvironment for windowScaleFactor ONLY; ActionFileReplay(layoutScale:) still gets the integer, because every action file coordinate was measured against it. Measured after the change with the display at 125%: scale factor -> 1.0 (change 1) then -> 1.25 (change 2), the same shape WinUI produces. The runtime-change half is a separate finding, see the next row."
2026-09-16,windows,gtk4,P42,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#80 on GtkBackend, the RUNTIME-CHANGE half, and the notification is not what is broken. After scui_window_display_scale() landed, the value was right at startup and then did not move: with the display changed from 125% to 100% under a running window, the probe called GetDpiForWindow DIRECTLY every two seconds -- no signal involved -- and reported 1.25 for 99 consecutive readings. Not stale by one sample; it never moved. So a WM_DPICHANGED subclass (also written, in the same file) is correct code that is structurally unreachable, and any amount of work on the notification would have changed nothing. THE CAUSE IS THE PROCESS DPI AWARENESS, which a second probe reads: GetAwarenessFromDpiAwarenessContext(GetWindowDpiAwarenessContext(hwnd)) returns 1 = SYSTEM_AWARE. Windows reports the system DPI as of process start to such a process and never sends it WM_DPICHANGED; that is by design, not a bug in GTK or here. AND GTK HAS A SWITCH FOR IT: gtk-4-1.dll carries the strings GDK_WIN32_PER_MONITOR_HIDPI, GDK_WIN32_DISABLE_HIDPI, _gdk_win32_enable_hidpi and WM_DPICHANGED. Relaunched with GDK_WIN32_PER_MONITOR_HIDPI=1, the same probe reads awareness=2 = PER_MONITOR_AWARE. So the platform CAN express it and GTK CAN be asked to; whether the value then follows a change is the next measurement, and it needs a person at the machine to move the slider. Do not enable it by default before measuring what it does to window size: every coordinate in testapp/actions/win/ was measured against the current geometry."
2026-09-16,windows,gtk4,P61,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#79 GTK 39px on Win-gtk4: FIXED and now driven, shortfall 0x0. P61 launched with SCUI_DEBUG, three probe points all agree -- content size: requested 620x420 allocated 620x420 shortfall 0x0, and the same at +250ms and +1500ms. The 2026-09-10 reading on this same app and backend was shortfall 0x39, so this is the same measurement with a different answer, not a different measurement. The supporting numbers are in the same log: titlebar allowance: defaultSize=620x420 +39 -> 459, and decoration probe BEFORE present: headerbar bare=47 titlebarClass=47 inWindow=47 gtkOwn=39 -- the 39 comes from probeGtkOwnDecorationHeight() measuring a real GtkHeaderBar in a realized window, not from a constant. Three probe points rather than one because a timing artefact shows two different numbers while a genuine no-op prints the same number twice. BLOCKED FIRST BY THE SINGLE-INSTANCE COLLISION and worth recording: P61 launched while P42 was up exited 0 with a completely empty log, because a SwiftPM executable has no metadata and every test app therefore shares the identifier com.example.SwiftCrossUIApp, which GApplication treats as one instance. An empty log reads exactly like an app that started and rendered nothing. Added SCUI_GTK_APP_ID to GtkBackend.init so two GTK test apps can run at once; this measurement was taken with P42 still running under it."
2026-09-16,windows,gtk4,P42,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#80 on GtkBackend, the runtime-change half: the value DOES follow, in per-monitor mode, and the user drove it. Launched with GDK_WIN32_PER_MONITOR_HIDPI=1 (awareness=2), the user changed the display 100% -> 150% -> 125% -> 100% without touching the window, and P42 recorded scale factor -> 1.0 (change 1), 1.5 (change 2), 1.25 (change 3), 1.0 (change 4), confirmed against what the user said they did. Win32 tracked it in the same seconds: windowDpi 96 -> 144 -> 120 -> 96, while systemDpi stayed 96 the whole time and GTK own widget_scale_factor stayed 1 across all three changes. Three numbers, three separate facts: GetDpiForSystem would have been the wrong call, GetDpiForWindow is the right one, and GTK integer scale never moved so notify::scale-factor could not have reported any of this. WHAT IS NOT YET SEPARATED: whether the WM_DPICHANGED subclass drove that recompute, or the resize did. In per-monitor mode Windows resizes the window on a DPI change, a resize runs a layout pass, a layout pass re-evaluates the body, and P42 records the scale from inside the body -- so a side effect produces the identical four lines. Same shape as the WinUI test where not touching the window was load-bearing. Control group added rather than argued: SCUI_GTK_NO_DPI_WATCH=1 skips installing the watch, and a DebugFeatures line logs WM_DPICHANGED -> scale before the handler runs, so ordering is visible. Two instances now run at once under SCUI_GTK_APP_ID, so one scale change answers it."
2026-09-16,windows,gtk4,P42,ok,n/a,window,unrecorded,"#80 on GtkBackend, CLOSED, and the control group deleted the code it was built to test. Two P42 instances at once (possible only because SCUI_GTK_APP_ID had just been added), both GDK_WIN32_PER_MONITOR_HIDPI=1, one with the WM_DPICHANGED subclass installed and one with SCUI_GTK_NO_DPI_WATCH=1 skipping it. The user moved the display 100% -> 150% -> 125%. BOTH recorded scale factor -> 1.0, 1.5, 1.25 at the SAME two timestamps (14:16:07 and 14:16:12), and the instrumented one logged no WM_DPICHANGED line at all. So the subclass drove nothing: Windows resizes a per-monitor-aware window on a DPI change, the resize runs a layout pass, and the layout pass recomputes the environment. The subclass, its C implementation, its header declaration, the Swift box that kept it alive and the control-group switch were all removed; a comment at setWindowEnvironmentChangeHandler records the measurement so it does not get added back. WHAT ACTUALLY FIXED IT is one line: enablePerMonitorDPIAwareness() calls g_setenv(GDK_WIN32_PER_MONITOR_HIDPI, 1, overwrite=false) before GTK initialises, which moves the process from SYSTEM_AWARE to PER_MONITOR_AWARE. Verified with no environment variable set: awareness=2, windowDpi=120, windowScaleFactor 1.25 at 125%. THE GEOMETRY COST IS ZERO, measured rather than assumed because every coordinate in testapp/actions/win/ depends on it: captured at 125% before the change in both modes and after the change as the default, 748x428 all three times. overwrite=false so GDK_WIN32_PER_MONITOR_HIDPI=0 still wins."
2026-09-16,mac,appkit,P57,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#117 LazyListRowLifetimes on AppKitBackend: DRIVEN AND PASSING, testapp/actions/mac/P57-scroll-the-whole-list.csv. NSTableView already reports this and nothing had asked: didAdd/didRemove on the row view. didRemove's own forRow: is documented as -1 when the row is no longer valid -- exactly the case the handler exists for -- so the index is recorded at didAdd instead; and a removal is not reported when the row is visible again already, because a reload can re-add before it removes and releasing a displayed row leaves a visible row nothing updates. Evidence is a new readout: DebugFeatures.liveLazyListRows, printed by P57 as 'rows held by the framework'. The conformance line above it is a claim about a TYPE; this counts the callback arriving. After dragging the scrollbar through all 500 rows the readout is 19 -- the visible window -- and the capture ends on rows 481-492 so the traversal is not in doubt. Without the callback it would be ~500: List.swift raises the LRU from 200 to a 4000 backstop for a conforming backend precisely because the callback is supposed to do the bounding. The final click on Select last (selection: 499) is there so a stale launch value could not be mistaken for a live one. SEPARATE DEFECT FOUND: wheel scrolling does nothing to this list (down) and blanks it entirely (up), on a control build with the release handler removed as well -- queue.md M6."
2026-09-16,ios,uikit,P57,ok,blocked,window,unrecorded,"#117 LazyListRowLifetimes on UIKitBackend: BUILT, CONFORMANCE LIVE, NOT DRIVEN AT SCALE. UITableViewDelegate.didEndDisplaying is the hook; UIKit hands the index over directly, unlike AppKit, so the only guard is that the row is not in indexPathsForVisibleRows already. The app reports 'row release reported: yes', so the conformance is visible to the framework at runtime and not just present in the file. What is missing is a traversal: 30 scroll rows through the XCUITest runner moved P57's list not at all -- the capture after them is pixel-identical to launch -- so the release count was never exercised and 'rows held by the framework' stayed at its launch value of 1. Not claimed as passing. The runner's scroll verb works elsewhere (P8, P27, P38), so the question is this app's view tree rather than the verb."
2026-09-16,android,android,P57,ok,partial,window,unrecorded,"#117 LazyListRowLifetimes on AndroidBackend: BUILT, CONFORMANCE LIVE, RELEASE OBSERVED AT SMALL SCALE ONLY. AbsListView.RecyclerListener.onMovedToScrapHeap is the hook, chosen over getView's convertView because the latter only fires when a scrapped view comes BACK -- views scrapped at the end of a scroll may never be reused, so the last screenful would be held for as long as nothing displaced it. The position is looked up in CustomListAdapter rather than carried, through two maps: a scrapped View arrives with nothing saying which row it held, and a position can be handed a new view while the old one is still on its way to the heap, which must answer -1 or a displayed row would be released. Observed: after launch (rows 0 and 1 visible) and a click on Select last (which jumps to 499), 'rows held by the framework' reads 2 -- so rows 0 and 1 were released. The margin is two rows, not four hundred: only about two rows fit on this emulator's list and the scroll rows did not move it, although they replay without error -- logcat shows the replay completing with all four actions, so the scroll verb is not throwing on this platform. Not claimed as passing at scale."
2026-09-16,ios,uikit,P71,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#121 on UIKit, the iPhone half: DRIVEN AND PASSING ON BOTH DEVICE KINDS with the same file, testapp/actions/ios/P71-shortcuts.csv. iPhone (the default swift-cross-ui simulator, iOS 27.0) plain 1, shifted 1, disabled 0 -- the same device that reported three zeros before today. iPad Pro 13-inch (M5) plain 1, shifted 1, disabled 0, which is the regression that mattered: a shortcut published twice fires twice. The gap was never the shortcut. Every UIKeyCommand this backend made lived inside buildMenu(with:), which the menu system calls with .main only where there is a menu bar, so on iPhone .commands and everything in it did nothing at all while the same binary worked on iPad. UIResponder.keyCommands publishes commands along the responder chain with no menu involved; ApplicationDelegate now overrides it, built in setApplicationMenu from a walk of the resolved menu (modifiedEnvironment is the case that carries the shortcut -- a walk that skipped it would find every item and no keys), and withheld once hasBuiltMainMenu is set, which buildMenu sets at the one place that proves a menu bar exists. Its closures live in FallbackShortcutActions rather than MenuShortcutActions because the two are rebuilt at different moments: beginRebuild inside buildMenu would silently invalidate every token this path was holding."
2026-09-17,windows,winui,P38,ok,n/a,window,default,"#P38 WebView2 on WinUI: ROOT-CAUSED, two independent defects, and the page renders when both are fixed. (1) THE APARTMENT: CoGetApartmentType read at three points -- before SwiftApplication.main() CO_E_NOTINITIALIZED, in onLaunched type 1 MTA, at core init type 1 MTA, no qualifier (not ASTA; the earlier CoInitializeEx probe could not tell MTA from ASTA). The 2026-09-16 row saying .multi -> .single was RUN AND REFUTED was itself wrong: there are three swift-winui checkouts (.build/checkouts, testapp/.compile-work-winui, .compile-work-gtk4), P38-WinUI builds from .compile-work-winui, and that row never said which was edited -- its positive control (Compiling WinUI = 2) proves a recompile, not that the edited copy was compiled. Edited in .compile-work-winui the apartment reads type 3 MAINSTA and EBWebView/ gains Default/ and Local State for the first time. (2) THE SUBCLASS: under STA the process then died with c0000374 heap corruption (WER Application Error 1000) about a second after core init. Bisected with env switches: no core init and no Source -> alive; Source only -> crash; ensureCore only -> crash; completed handler and Source callback both skipped -> still crash. Stack from cdb (WinDbg 1.2606 IS installed via winget; an earlier claim that this machine had no debugger checked only PATH and Windows Kits) : swiftCore!swift_retainCount reading f777f777 freed-memory fill <- P38_WinUI <- Microsoft_UI_Xaml_Controls com_ptr<IWeakReferenceSource>::release_ref <- WebView2::RegisterCoreEventHandlers <- CreateCoreWebViewFromEnvironment. The control takes a weak reference to its OUTER object; a Swift subclass makes that the Swift wrapper and the release frees it. Fixed by making WebViewWidget a Grid that holds an unsubclassed WebView2. RESULT under .single: browser process started, navigated to about:blank then https://example.com/, Example Domain drawn, Navigations reported: 2 (p38-grid-sta-20260917-064727.png). CONTROL: the same Grid widget under .multi stayed type 1 and never completed in 45 s, so both halves are required. The .single edit to the dependency checkout was reverted and made read-only again; a durable way to get STA is still open."
2026-09-17,windows,winui,P38,ok,n/a,window,default,"#P38 WebView2 on WinUI: FIXED without touching the dependency. WinUIBackend now starts the application itself, single-threaded -- WinUIApplication.runSingleThreaded() in WinUIApplication+SingleThreaded.swift: WindowsAppRuntimeInitializer(threadingModel: .single), Application.start, and a copy of swift-winui's run-loop tickler (internal upstream, so unreachable; kept behaviourally identical). The swift-winui checkout is back at .multi and read-only. Measured with the dependency untouched: apartment CO_E_NOTINITIALIZED before main, type 3 MAINSTA in onLaunched and at core init, the browser process started, navigated to about:blank then https://example.com/, RENDER COMPLETE. CONTROL: SCUI_WINUI_MTA=1 takes the old path and reads type 1, no completion, no navigation. REGRESSION, because this changes startup for every WinUI app: all 22 apps in window-sizes-winui-20260916.csv2 rebuilt (22/22, 0 errors) and swept -- 22/22 window sizes identical to the MTA baseline (window-sizes-winui-sta-20260917.csv2). Window size does not prove the copied tickler works, since a broken tickler silently stops DispatchQueue.main and main-actor work, so RENDER COMPLETE markers were compared too: no app has the marker under MTA and lacks it under STA; six have it in neither run (P5 P40 P47 P50 P53 P54) and P11 and P46 gained it."
2026-09-17,windows,winui,P36,ok,n/a,window,default,"#128 fractional padding: VERIFIED on both Windows backends, measured by colour rather than read from a screenshot. P36 gained three identical 40x40 squares padded 8, 8.5 and 9 inside a pure-blue background; PIL finds each square by its colour and walks outward over blue. WinUI (p36-padding-20260917-072054.png) and GtkBackend (p36-padding-gtk4-20260917-072351.png) give the same numbers: inner 40x40 on all three, blue boxes 56x56, 57x57, 58x58. 57 is the assertion -- no integer padding produces it, so 8.5 survives from the modifier to the widget. The half pixel lands as left 9 right 8, top 9 bottom 8, identically on both backends. P36's on-screen missing-API line was one-third stale and was narrowed: padding takes Double (PaddingModifier.swift:9); cornerRadius (CornerRadiusModifier.swift:2) and HStack spacing (HStack.swift:19) still take Int, so they stay listed. [Row split from a single windows/winui+gtk4 entry on 2026-09-17 so it reaches a column: coverage.zsh keys on platform/backend and has no combined column, so the original was appended, committed, and displayed nowhere -- check_results_columns caught it on merge. The measurement covered both backends and both captures are named in it, so the text is unchanged and stands for each.]"
2026-09-17,windows,gtk4,P36,ok,n/a,window,default,"#128 fractional padding: VERIFIED on both Windows backends, measured by colour rather than read from a screenshot. P36 gained three identical 40x40 squares padded 8, 8.5 and 9 inside a pure-blue background; PIL finds each square by its colour and walks outward over blue. WinUI (p36-padding-20260917-072054.png) and GtkBackend (p36-padding-gtk4-20260917-072351.png) give the same numbers: inner 40x40 on all three, blue boxes 56x56, 57x57, 58x58. 57 is the assertion -- no integer padding produces it, so 8.5 survives from the modifier to the widget. The half pixel lands as left 9 right 8, top 9 bottom 8, identically on both backends. P36's on-screen missing-API line was one-third stale and was narrowed: padding takes Double (PaddingModifier.swift:9); cornerRadius (CornerRadiusModifier.swift:2) and HStack spacing (HStack.swift:19) still take Int, so they stay listed. [Row split from a single windows/winui+gtk4 entry on 2026-09-17 so it reaches a column: coverage.zsh keys on platform/backend and has no combined column, so the original was appended, committed, and displayed nowhere -- check_results_columns caught it on merge. The measurement covered both backends and both captures are named in it, so the text is unchanged and stands for each.]"
2026-09-17,windows,winui,P65,ok,n/a,window,default,"#32 magnify and rotate: NOT YET DRIVEN, and the reason is measured. New tool testapp/touch_gesture.c (+ touch_gesture.zsh) injects two-finger pinch or rotate, or a one-finger drag, through CreateSyntheticPointerDevice(PT_TOUCH) and InjectSyntheticPointerInput, frame-relative physical pixels, printing every injection's result. It is a separate tool rather than an action-file verb because InputAction is switched over exhaustively by three synthesisers and the AppKit one cannot be compiled here. Against P65-WinUI (STA build): pinch on the magnify target, 22 injections all ok, no magnify line in p65-debug-events.log; one-finger drag on the drag target, all ok, nothing. REACHABILITY CONTROL that involves no app code: a one-finger touch drag on P65's title bar left GetWindowRect at 130,130 636x599 -- the window did not move. So touch injection is accepted by the API and delivered nowhere, system-wide, which says nothing yet about the gesture implementations. enable_input.zsh --check reports the remote-desktop host that coincided with refused mouse injection on 2026-09-16; mouse runs succeeded then only right after the user interacted with the session. Next: repeat the title-bar control first in such a window; only if the window moves does a silent P65 mean a backend defect."
2026-09-16,mac,appkit,P57,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#117 LazyListRowLifetimes on AppKitBackend, RE-MEASURED with a readout that does not need a scroll distance argued about: 'rows built / held by the framework: 38 / 19'. Built is monotonic since launch, held is what the framework still has, so 19 rows were released. The difference cannot be the LRU instead: List.swift caps a non-conforming backend at 200 and a conforming one at 4000, and 38 is far below both. Only 38 were ever built because NSTableView coalesces layout during a scrollbar drag and renders the landing position, not the positions passed over -- a finer drag (51 steps instead of 17) produced the identical 38/19, which is itself the answer to 'did the traversal matter'. A ticker under --debug now re-renders the readouts twice a second, so the number in a capture is live rather than whatever the last state change left there."
2026-09-16,ios,uikit,P57,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#117 LazyListRowLifetimes on UIKitBackend: DRIVEN AND PASSING, testapp/actions/ios/P57-walk-the-list.csv. 'rows built / held by the framework: 500 / 6' -- every row in the list was built and six are still held, so 494 were released. Supersedes the row recorded earlier today as BUILT NOT DRIVEN: that reading was wrong for a reason in the action file, not in the backend. The scroll column is WHEEL NOTCHES and a notch is 40 points on this runner, so the 'scroll 0,400' those first attempts used asked for a 16,000-point drag whose destination was far outside the window; thirty of them moved the screen not one pixel and the capture afterwards was identical to launch, which reads as a list that cannot be scrolled. Six notches per row scrolls it. Mistakes entry 17."
2026-09-16,android,android,P57,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#117 LazyListRowLifetimes on AndroidBackend: DRIVEN AND PASSING, testapp/actions/android/P57-walk-the-list.csv. 'rows built / held by the framework: 190 / 3' -- 187 rows released, and the non-conforming LRU cap of 200 would not have evicted anything at 190, so the difference is the callback. Supersedes both earlier rows today. The first said the scroll verb did not move the list; it was a 300-notch row, which at 40 points times density 2.625 is a 31,500-pixel drag on a 2,400-pixel screen. The second claimed the release was observed because 'held' read 2 after clicking Select last -- that claim was wrong and is withdrawn: setSelectedItem uses setItemChecked and does not scroll, so row 499 was never displayed and nothing was released. Two separate readings of the same nothing. Mistakes entry 17."
2026-09-17,mac,appkit,P57,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"#117 LazyListRowLifetimes on AppKitBackend, RE-MEASURED once the wheel worked: 'rows built / held by the framework: 500 / 19' -- every row built, nineteen held, 481 released -- and the capture ends on rows 481-490. Supersedes today's earlier 38 / 19, which was a scrollbar drag: NSTableView coalesces layout during a drag and renders the landing position, so only two screenfuls were ever built. testapp/actions/mac/P57-scroll-the-whole-list.csv now scrolls with the wheel. The difference cannot be the LRU: List.swift caps a non-conforming backend at 200 and a conforming one at 4000."
2026-09-17,mac,appkit,P57,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"queue M6 CLOSED, and it was never an AppKit defect. Two defects in AppKitSynthesiser.postScroll produced one silence. (1) The wheel delta's sign was inverted: dy is positive downwards in the action-file format, NSEvent's scrolling delta is the finger's direction, so every downward scroll scrolled up. (2) The compensation that exists for views which ignore a synthesised event ran unconditionally, because NSScrollView applies a wheel event on a LATER pass and the synchronous check therefore always read 'unchanged' -- so it moved the view down by exactly what the event had just moved it up, both in units of lineScroll. Net travel zero, which is indistinguishable from a list that ignores the wheel. Scrolling UP at the top drew no rows for a third reason: contentView.scroll(to:) accepts a point above the document and AppKit's own handling clamps where this path did not. Fixed: sign corrected, the fallback pumps the runloop 50ms before deciding, the target is clamped, and postScroll now reports hit view, scroll view, origins and range on stderr -- the line 'before=(0,192) afterEvent=(0,0)' is what opened it. Mistakes entry 18. Seven mac action files use scroll rows; four were re-run clean, P8 and P4 crash for pre-existing reasons unrelated to this (ForEach exclusivity; AnyWidget type mismatch) and neither had ever been driven on mac."
2026-09-17,windows,winui,P65,ok,ok,window,default,"#32 magnify, rotate and drag: DRIVEN with real synthetic touch on BOTH Windows backends, SUPERSEDING the inconclusive row of the same morning. The difference was the session, not the apps: with the user connected over Google Remote Desktop, a one-finger touch drag on P65's title bar moved the window by exactly the drag (frame 78,78 -> 158,118 for +80,+40), where earlier it had not moved at all. ASUS software (Armoury Crate, AsusPTPService, GlideX and others) was running in BOTH runs, so it is not what changed. WinUIBackend, first pass: drag (180,20) -> translation (173,23); pinch 40->160 px gap -> 3.478; rotate 90 deg -> 1.531 rad -- and three defects. (1) Handlers were added in update*GestureTarget on every update and never removed: ONE drag logged drag ended 12 times, the next pinch magnify ENDED 18 times, the rotate after it 23 times. (2) The isEnabled guard returned early and left old handlers live. (3) startLocation was hard-coded to zero and location was the raw translation. Fixed with ManipulationTarget, a Grid (Border is final in the binding) that subscribes once in init; update swaps closures; disabling clears them; the start comes from ManipulationStarted.position. After: each ENDED exactly once, drag start (22,45) -- the target-local point computed beforehand -- rotate 90 deg -> 1.571 rad. GtkBackend: drag translation (180,20) start (36,45), magnify 4.001, each once -- but rotate ENDED -0.000 rad. It read `angle` (the absolute angle of the line between the fingers; the log started at -4.752 and wrapped) instead of `angle_delta`, and negated it per a comment that holds in AppKit's y-up space but not GTK's y-down one. Then angle_delta proved normalised to [0, 2pi): -45 deg reported 5.498. WinUI reports the same injections as -0.772 and a 200-degree turn as 3.515, signed and unbounded, so GTK now unwraps step by step, reset on begin. After: +90 -> 1.571, -45 -> -0.785, +200 -> 3.514, against WinUI 1.571, -0.772, 3.515."
2026-09-17,windows,gtk4,P65,ok,ok,window,default,"#32 magnify, rotate and drag: DRIVEN with real synthetic touch on BOTH Windows backends, SUPERSEDING the inconclusive row of the same morning. The difference was the session, not the apps: with the user connected over Google Remote Desktop, a one-finger touch drag on P65's title bar moved the window by exactly the drag (frame 78,78 -> 158,118 for +80,+40), where earlier it had not moved at all. ASUS software (Armoury Crate, AsusPTPService, GlideX and others) was running in BOTH runs, so it is not what changed. WinUIBackend, first pass: drag (180,20) -> translation (173,23); pinch 40->160 px gap -> 3.478; rotate 90 deg -> 1.531 rad -- and three defects. (1) Handlers were added in update*GestureTarget on every update and never removed: ONE drag logged drag ended 12 times, the next pinch magnify ENDED 18 times, the rotate after it 23 times. (2) The isEnabled guard returned early and left old handlers live. (3) startLocation was hard-coded to zero and location was the raw translation. Fixed with ManipulationTarget, a Grid (Border is final in the binding) that subscribes once in init; update swaps closures; disabling clears them; the start comes from ManipulationStarted.position. After: each ENDED exactly once, drag start (22,45) -- the target-local point computed beforehand -- rotate 90 deg -> 1.571 rad. GtkBackend: drag translation (180,20) start (36,45), magnify 4.001, each once -- but rotate ENDED -0.000 rad. It read `angle` (the absolute angle of the line between the fingers; the log started at -4.752 and wrapped) instead of `angle_delta`, and negated it per a comment that holds in AppKit's y-up space but not GTK's y-down one. Then angle_delta proved normalised to [0, 2pi): -45 deg reported 5.498. WinUI reports the same injections as -0.772 and a 200-degree turn as 3.515, signed and unbounded, so GTK now unwraps step by step, reset on begin. After: +90 -> 1.571, -45 -> -0.785, +200 -> 3.514, against WinUI 1.571, -0.772, 3.515."
2026-09-17,windows,winui,P41,ok,ok,window,default,"#P41 date pickers on Windows, both backends, DRIVEN. WRITE-BACK: clicking the 13th in the .graphical calendar logged `.graphical -> 2025-08-13` on WinUIBackend (actions/win/P41-graphical-pick-day.csv) and on GtkBackend (P41-graphical-pick-day-gtk4.csv); the bound day was 24. P41 gained an event log for this -- a line per binding change -- because a CalendarView highlights a clicked day whether or not anything is written back. FOUND ON THE WAY, the shape the repo CLAUDE.md forbids and worse: WinUIBackend printed `.hourMinuteAndSecond is not supported in WinUIBackend. Falling back to .hourAndMinute.` on every update, and at LAUNCH with no input wrote the bound 09:46:40 back as 09:46:00 (.hourMinuteAndSecond) and 09:46 (.hourAndMinute). Cause: programmatic TimePicker.selectedTime raises timeChanged, TimePicker truncates seconds, and the echo went to the binding as an edit; date edits also took their time from TimePicker and zeroed seconds. GtkBackend showed H:M:S spin buttons with 09:46:40 and no launch writes. FIX: a 00-59 selector beside the TimePicker; an isApplyingUpdate guard plus an equality check so programmatic updates never report; hour/minute and date edits keep the bound seconds. The first build sized the selector with naturalSize(of: ComboBox), which measured its 60-item list and made the window 2757 px tall; now a fixed 72x32. AFTER: launch log has RENDER COMPLETE and nothing else, 0 not-supported lines, the selector shows 40, text 09:46:40, window 942x689. Editing seconds by touch (mouse was refused by then) logged exactly `.hourMinuteAndSecond -> 2025-08-24 09:46:41`. NOT DRIVEN: an hour/minute edit keeping the seconds -- that path is in code, not measured. P11, the other DatePicker app, rebuilt: RENDER COMPLETE, no errors, 762x652 as in the baseline."
2026-09-17,windows,gtk4,P41,ok,ok,window,default,"#P41 date pickers on Windows, both backends, DRIVEN. WRITE-BACK: clicking the 13th in the .graphical calendar logged `.graphical -> 2025-08-13` on WinUIBackend (actions/win/P41-graphical-pick-day.csv) and on GtkBackend (P41-graphical-pick-day-gtk4.csv); the bound day was 24. P41 gained an event log for this -- a line per binding change -- because a CalendarView highlights a clicked day whether or not anything is written back. FOUND ON THE WAY, the shape the repo CLAUDE.md forbids and worse: WinUIBackend printed `.hourMinuteAndSecond is not supported in WinUIBackend. Falling back to .hourAndMinute.` on every update, and at LAUNCH with no input wrote the bound 09:46:40 back as 09:46:00 (.hourMinuteAndSecond) and 09:46 (.hourAndMinute). Cause: programmatic TimePicker.selectedTime raises timeChanged, TimePicker truncates seconds, and the echo went to the binding as an edit; date edits also took their time from TimePicker and zeroed seconds. GtkBackend showed H:M:S spin buttons with 09:46:40 and no launch writes. FIX: a 00-59 selector beside the TimePicker; an isApplyingUpdate guard plus an equality check so programmatic updates never report; hour/minute and date edits keep the bound seconds. The first build sized the selector with naturalSize(of: ComboBox), which measured its 60-item list and made the window 2757 px tall; now a fixed 72x32. AFTER: launch log has RENDER COMPLETE and nothing else, 0 not-supported lines, the selector shows 40, text 09:46:40, window 942x689. Editing seconds by touch (mouse was refused by then) logged exactly `.hourMinuteAndSecond -> 2025-08-24 09:46:41`. NOT DRIVEN: an hour/minute edit keeping the seconds -- that path is in code, not measured. P11, the other DatePicker app, rebuilt: RENDER COMPLETE, no errors, 762x652 as in the baseline."
2026-09-17,windows,winui,P41,ok,ok,window,default,"#P41 WinUIBackend H:M:S, KEYBOARD paths, and the one path the previous row called not driven. Keys sent with a scratch SendInput tool kept OUT of the repo at the user's request; it prints the foreground window before sending, because keys go only there -- it read `P41 date picker styles` every time. The window was activated by a synthetic touch tap, since mouse injection was refused. (A) tap opens the seconds list, Down, Return -> `.hourMinuteAndSecond -> 2025-08-24 09:46:41`. (B) Escape closes the list keeping focus, no line; Down on the CLOSED box -> `09:46:42`. (C) tap open, Down, Escape -> no new line, and the capture shows 42 with the keyboard focus ring on the box, so the keys did reach it and Escape cancelled. (D) HOUR EDIT KEEPS SECONDS: tap the TimePicker hour, Down, Return -> `10:46:42` -- hour 9 -> 10, minutes and the bound 42 seconds unchanged. That was the path the previous row listed as in code but not driven; it is driven now. A FIRST ATTEMPT AT B AND C WAS INVALID and is recorded so nobody reuses it: between runs the .graphical CalendarView grew a sixth week row, the window went from 689 to 741 px tall, the seconds box moved from y=624 to y=676, and taps at the old point landed on the label -- so its `0 new lines` proved nothing. Caught from the capture, not the log."
2026-09-17,mac,appkit,P4,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"P4 CRASHED AT LAUNCH on macOS and now runs: 'AnyWidget used with incompatible widget type NSTextField; actual widget type is AppKitHitTestingContainer'. The cause is that TextField stopped being an elementary view when TextFieldStyle was opened up -- its body is AnyView(style.makeView(...)), so the widget on its view-graph node is a container and the .inspect overload's widget.into() as NSTextField traps. P4's own inspect closure is EMPTY on macOS (everything in it is behind #if canImport(WinUIBackend)), so the crash came from a modifier that had nothing to do. Fixed by making AppKitBackend's concrete-type inspect overloads SEARCH the subtree for the requested NSView subclass instead of casting the node's widget: a strict generalisation, since a widget that is already the requested type is returned by the first line. When the search finds nothing it still traps, but names the type it wanted and prints the actual view tree. Driven: actions/mac/P4-rows-callbacks-and-scrolling.csv, 22 actions, and the app reports Rows: 250, rows 200-249, update tick: 4, callbacks: 3. Same defect shape exists in UIKitBackend and WinUIBackend inspection modifiers -- queue."
2026-09-17,mac,appkit,P8,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"P8 CRASHED AT LAUNCH on macOS and now runs: 'Simultaneous accesses to 0x..., but modification requires exclusive access', with BOTH accesses reported at ForEach.commit + 1388 -- the same function at the same offset, entered twice. The stack shows the re-entry: ForEach.commit -> LayoutSystem.commitStackLayout -> closure #2 in layoutableChild -> AnyViewGraphNode.commit -> ViewGraphNode.commit -> ForEach.commit. The held access was cache: &children.stackLayoutCache: an inout on a CLASS property holds an exclusive access for the whole call, and that call commits every child. Fixed with ForEachViewChildren.withStackLayoutCache, which copies the cache out, passes the copy inout, and writes it back -- so a re-entrant pass works on its own copy. A cache rebuilt a pass later is the cost; a process that stops is not. Applied to all four sites (two in computeLayout, two in commit), because the other three are the same hazard reached by a different path. Driven: actions/mac/P8-nested-scroll-views.csv, 18 actions. Regression sweep: P0 P2 P13 P16 P22 P23 P34 P57 all launch and survive, P34 and P23 replay their action files unchanged, Scripts/test.sh green."
2026-09-17,ios,uikit,P4,ok,ok,window,unrecorded,"P4 CRASHED AT LAUNCH on the simulator for the same reason as macOS and now runs: 'AnyWidget used with incompatible widget type WrapperWidget<UITextField>; actual widget type is BaseViewWidget'. TextField is no longer an elementary view -- its body is AnyView(style.makeView(...)) since TextFieldStyle was opened up -- so the node's widget is a BaseViewWidget and inspectAsWrapperWidget's widget.into() traps. P4's inspect closure is EMPTY on this platform, so the crash came from a modifier with nothing to do. Fixed the same way as AppKitBackend: the concrete-type inspect overloads now search the view subtree for the requested UIView subclass instead of casting, which is a strict generalisation, and trap with the type wanted plus the actual tree when the search finds nothing. Verified by launching P4 on the simulator: it renders 'Rows: 80, rows 0-49, update tick: 0, callbacks: 0' and the log carries no fatal error. WinUIBackend has the same shape and is the one machine that cannot be checked from here -- queue M8."
2026-09-17,ios,uikit,P65,ok,ok,window,default,"#32 magnify and rotate DRIVEN on iOS for the first time, through two new action-file verbs. Three consecutive runs, all with drag events: 0 -- so neither gesture became a drag. magnify ENDED read 1.625 every time; rotate ENDED read 0.995, 0.995 and 1.233 rad. BOTH LEFT THEIR STARTING VALUES (1.00 and 0), which is what P65 asks; NEITHER MATCHES WHAT THE ROW REQUESTED (scale 2.0 and 45 degrees = 0.785 rad) and the rotation is not even repeatable, so XCUITest delivers a gesture of its own size around the asked-for one. That is a fact about the driver, not about UIKitBackend, and nothing here has yet measured backend fidelity. Captures p65-ios-final-20260917-113959.png, -114256.png and -115535.png. Three earlier captures said magnify: (none yet) and NONE of them was a backend defect. XCUIElement.pinch/rotate act on an ELEMENT and an action file names none, so the runner first used the window: its centre sat on the rotate panel, so rotate registered and the pinch, sent to the same point, had no magnify recogniser under it. Scrolling the wanted panel under the centre was the second wrong answer -- P65 has nothing to scroll, so the scroll rows became drags and the drag panel logged them. The third: aiming by the move pointer and hit-testing, which found a tree in a coordinate space that is not the screen (frames 500 wide at x=-36 inside a 440-point window, the panel under the point absent). Waiting does not clear that -- eight queries over four seconds, no change -- but one delivered tap does, and the query straight after it put the panel at {{0,332},{267,62}}. The file therefore opens with a click on inert background; the runner drops candidates outside the window and says so in the log when only a container is left."
2026-09-17,windows,gtk4,P38,ok,n/a,window,default,"#P38 on GtkBackend (Windows -gtk4): FAILS, measured rather than read off the source. The P38 WebView2 fix of the same day is WinUIBackend-only, and GtkBackend+WebView.swift is still a Gtk.Label placeholder. Run: builds, launches, RENDER COMPLETE, no crash, process stays up for 25 s. The framed area shows the text `WebView is not available in this build (GtkBackend has no WebKitGTK)` and the app reports `Navigations reported: 0` (p38-gtk4-test-20260917-082518.png). This is the shape the repo CLAUDE.md rules out on a shipped backend -- a truthful report of a missing feature, not the feature. The file's own 2026-09-04 decision records the route: WebKitGTK has no Windows port (nothing under C:/gtk4), so Windows GTK must host WebView2 as a child of the GTK surface HWND, while Linux/WSL would take webkitgtk-6.0 as a conditional dependency."
2026-09-17,windows,gtk4,P38,ok,ok,window,default,"#P38 on GtkBackend (Windows -gtk4): FIXED, the placeholder replaced by a real WebView2, and driven. Before (same day, row above): placeholder text, Navigations reported: 0. Implementation: Sources/GtkCHelpers/gtk_webview2.c hosts WebView2 as a child of the GTK window's HWND, over a GtkLabel that GTK lays out; every 50 ms it maps the widget's bounds into the HWND client area (root position + gtk_native_get_surface_transform for the CSD shadow, times gdk_surface_get_scale) and reports SourceChanged back. WebView2.h is a compile-time include added by compile.zsh only when present; WebView2Loader.dll is loaded at runtime with LoadLibraryW, so neither is a link dependency and a machine without them still builds and shows a labelled reason. install_gtk4_windows.zsh fetches both from the official NuGet package, pinned 1.0.3179.45. RESULTS: example.com drawn inside the frame, aligned to its border; `navigated to https://example.com/`; RENDER COMPLETE. BOUNDS TRACKING: after a navigation added a line to the app's list, the frame moved 8 px down (top y=258 -> 266) and the browser followed it. USER NAVIGATION INSIDE THE PAGE, by keyboard (click the page to focus, Tab, Return): `navigated to https://www.iana.org/help/example-domains` and the IANA page drawn, Navigations reported: 2. The same keyboard sequence on P38-WinUI also logged `navigated to https://www.iana.org/help/example-domains`, closing the gap noted there. NOT WORKING, recorded: a synthetic TOUCH tap on the Learn more link moved the pointer onto it but did not follow the link, on GTK; keyboard was used instead. FOUND ON THE WAY: install_gtk4_windows.zsh could not be re-run on an installed prefix -- patch --forward wrote 302 .rej files into lib/pkgconfig and the check found the build prefix inside them and exited 1, before any later step ran. The rejects created by that run were deleted; the script now skips relocation when no .pc references the build prefix and checks only *.pc."
2026-09-17,windows,winui,P38,ok,ok,window,default,"#P38 touch tap on an in-page link, both Windows backends: ROOT-CAUSED AND FIXED, and the cause was the test tool, not WebView2. The morning row recorded a synthetic touch tap on Learn more moving the pointer but not following the link. Same tap on P38-WinUI also failed, so it was not GTK hosting. A touch SWIPE did scroll the IANA page, so touch reached Chromium. A scratch tapper then clicked the link, and a first variant table pointed at POINTER_FLAG_PRIMARY|FIRSTBUTTON -- REFUTED by an interleaved repeat where the plain variant also succeeded. Interleaved 4x4 on WinUI P38: repo touch_gesture.zsh 0/4; scratch taps with no hold, 60 ms hold, or PRIMARY flags 4/4 each. A scratch replica of the repo tool timing, including only 30 ms between UP and DestroySyntheticPointerDevice, went 3/3, refuting that too. The tool's own output gave it away: `drag 230 421 230 421 1 30` printed 32 steps. touch_gesture.c grouped drag with rotate, which has a fifth number, so drag's [steps] was read from the [ms] slot -- 30 steps at the default 16 ms, a ~500 ms press-and-hold. Chromium treated it as a long press. XAML's ComboBox accepted the long press, which is why the P41 touch taps looked like taps. Fixed: only rotate takes the fifth argument. After: 3 steps printed; WinUI P38 link tap 3/3; GTK P38 link tap 3/3 (`navigated to https://www.iana.org/help/example-domains` each round). The P65 drag measurement stands: its endpoints were right, only the step count differed (16 instead of 20)."
2026-09-17,windows,gtk4,P38,ok,ok,window,default,"#P38 touch tap on an in-page link, both Windows backends: ROOT-CAUSED AND FIXED, and the cause was the test tool, not WebView2. The morning row recorded a synthetic touch tap on Learn more moving the pointer but not following the link. Same tap on P38-WinUI also failed, so it was not GTK hosting. A touch SWIPE did scroll the IANA page, so touch reached Chromium. A scratch tapper then clicked the link, and a first variant table pointed at POINTER_FLAG_PRIMARY|FIRSTBUTTON -- REFUTED by an interleaved repeat where the plain variant also succeeded. Interleaved 4x4 on WinUI P38: repo touch_gesture.zsh 0/4; scratch taps with no hold, 60 ms hold, or PRIMARY flags 4/4 each. A scratch replica of the repo tool timing, including only 30 ms between UP and DestroySyntheticPointerDevice, went 3/3, refuting that too. The tool's own output gave it away: `drag 230 421 230 421 1 30` printed 32 steps. touch_gesture.c grouped drag with rotate, which has a fifth number, so drag's [steps] was read from the [ms] slot -- 30 steps at the default 16 ms, a ~500 ms press-and-hold. Chromium treated it as a long press. XAML's ComboBox accepted the long press, which is why the P41 touch taps looked like taps. Fixed: only rotate takes the fifth argument. After: 3 steps printed; WinUI P38 link tap 3/3; GTK P38 link tap 3/3 (`navigated to https://www.iana.org/help/example-domains` each round). The P65 drag measurement stands: its endpoints were right, only the step count differed (16 instead of 20)."
2026-09-17,wsl,gtk4,P38,ok,n/a,window,default,"#P38 on GtkBackend, Linux/WSL: WebKitGTK 6.0 implemented, fallback driven, REAL PAGE NOT YET DRIVEN. Sources/GtkCHelpers/gtk_webkit.c loads libwebkitgtk-6.0.so.4 with dlopen and appends a WebKitWebView to the host box, reporting notify::uri. dlopen keeps the 2026-09-04 constraint: no header, no link flag, so the Windows GTK build and a Linux machine without the package are unchanged. The host became a GtkBox, since a label cannot hold the web view; the Windows WebView2 path was re-verified after that change (page drawn, link tap 3/3). WSL: synced to ~/proj, compile.zsh P38 built with `Compiling GtkCHelpers gtk_webkit.c`, launched, RENDER COMPLETE, and with the package absent (apt: Installed none, needs sudo) the frame reads `WebKitGTK could not start: libwebkitgtk-6.0.so.4 could not be loaded (... No such file or directory); install libwebkitgtk-6.0-4 (sudo apt install libwebkitgtk-6.0-4)` (p38-wslg-nowebkit-20260917-143404.png). Rendering a page on WSL waits for the user to install libwebkitgtk-6.0-4."
2026-09-17,android,android,P65,ok,ok,window,default,"#32 magnify and rotate DRIVEN on Android, and the drive found a real backend defect. The synthesiser now builds a two-contact stream itself: MotionEvent.obtain with PointerProperties/PointerCoords arrays, ACTION_POINTER_DOWN/UP carrying the pointer index in the action high bits, contacts laid out 40 points either side of the last move row. actions/android/P65-pinch-and-rotate.csv, capture p65-android-final-20260917-143345.png: magnify ENDED 2.000 for a row asking 200, rotate ENDED 0.785 rad for a row asking 45 degrees, drag events 0. Exact, unlike iOS where XCUITest delivers a gesture of its own size. THE DEFECT: before the fix the same file reported magnify ENDED 1.194 and rotate ENDED 0.633 rad, twice, identically. Not noise and not the synthesiser -- a scrolling ancestor takes the gesture at one touch slop of travel (8dp = 21 px at 420 dpi) and ContinuousGestureContainer reports the resulting ACTION_CANCEL as an end, after which every later move falls through its tracking guard in silence. 1.194 is step 12 of 62, where the first contact has moved 20.3 px and step 13 would move it 22.0; 0.633 rad is step 25 of 31, where that contact has moved 20.4 px horizontally. Two gestures, two fractions, one threshold. Fixed by requestDisallowInterceptTouchEvent(true) on ACTION_DOWN, which is what a child that owns a gesture is supposed to say; a finger inside any scrollable area hit this exactly as the synthesiser did. Also measured: --no-showtime photographs a replay in flight -- the final capture is taken right after the 5s one, and these gestures run for seconds, so the first Android run read partial values that were not wrong, just early."
2026-09-17,mac,appkit,P69,ok,n/a,window,default,"#123 re-probed after the other machine found a GTK Close button still exposing an X child through AT-SPI. macOS is clean, and now provably: ax_dump grew a --children flag and prints what sits UNDER each named button. P69 gives AXButton desc=Close (no children), desc=Delete help=Removes the file permanently (no children), desc=Volume value=40 percent (no children), and no AXStaticText X or decorative anywhere. The flat listing it printed before could not have shown a child; this is the same measurement asked a question it could not answer before."
2026-09-17,android,android,P69,ok,n/a,window,default,"#123 re-probed, and the uncompressed tree does show what GTK showed: Button desc=Close has a child chain ending in TextView text=X, and the decorative TextView is present. That is NOT the defect, and the source already said so: uiautomator plain dump sets FLAG_INCLUDE_NOT_IMPORTANT_VIEWS and lists views no screen reader reaches. The compressed dump, which is the one that matches what an AT walks, gives Button desc=Close with 0 children, Delete and Volume likewise, and no decorative node at all. Both dumps taken on emulator-5554 this run so the difference is the flag and not the build."
2026-09-17,ios,uikit,P69,ok,ok,window,default,"#123 on iOS had NO external probe until today, and adding one found a real gap. The XCUITest runner now prints the accessibility tree with --dump-tree (test_ios.zsh), which resolves the same tree an assistive technology walks; P69 iOS evidence until now was the app describing its own views, which is exactly where a label on a wrapper nobody visits still reads as correct. First dump: the ENTIRE app resolved to one StaticText, and it belonged to the harness own actualView button. Every SwiftCrossUI Text was an unnamed Other -- VoiceOver had nothing to say about any text in any app on this backend. Cause: UIKitBackend.TextView draws through TextKit, so it is a plain UIView and not an accessibility element. Fixed by publishing isAccessibilityElement/accessibilityLabel whenever the text is set, plus the staticText trait, and by teaching namedChild to accept TextView so .accessibilityLabel reaches it. After: seven StaticTexts with their content as labels, Close/Delete/Volume still carry their names with NO X child, and decorative appears nowhere. Captures p69-ios-final-20260917-150034.png; action file actions/ios/P69-read-the-tree.csv."
2026-09-17,android,android,P38,ok,ok,window,default,"#P38 revalidated on the three backends here after the WebView2 work landed on the other machine. macOS and iOS render example.com and report Navigations reported: 1. Android rendered the page and reported 0 -- CustomWebView reported only from shouldOverrideUrlLoading, which Android asks about navigations the PAGE starts, so the first load, started by our own loadUrl, was never reported at all. Moved to onPageStarted, which fires for every navigation whoever starts it and is what WKWebView didCommit does on the two Apple backends; Android now reports 1 and lists the URL. Captures p38-macos-final-20260917-143715.png, p38-ios-final-20260917-143937.png, p38-android-final-20260917-144742.png (before: -144342.png, the same page with 0 beside it)."
2026-09-17,wsl,gtk4,P38,ok,ok,window,default,"#P38 on GtkBackend, WSL: WORKS, driven end to end. libwebkitgtk-6.0-4 installed as WSL root (apt-get rc=0) and added to install_tool_wsl.zsh. FIRST RUN CRASHED THE APP: WebKit's UI process printed `Could not create surfaceless EGL display: EGL_NOT_INITIALIZED. Aborting...` with no Mesa variables AND with GALLIUM_DRIVER=d3d12 (hw); WEBKIT_DISABLE_DMABUF_RENDERER=1 and WEBKIT_DISABLE_COMPOSITING_MODE=1 did not prevent it; LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1 GALLIUM_DRIVER=llvmpipe (sw) drew the page. FIX in gtk_webkit.c: before creating the web view, probe eglGetPlatformDisplay(EGL_PLATFORM_SURFACELESS_MESA)+eglInitialize via dlopen; if it fails, set LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1 GALLIUM_DRIVER=llvmpipe and probe again; if that fails too, do not create the web view and show the reason -- a GPU Linux desktop is left untouched. SECOND BUG: the page drew but `Navigations reported: 0`, because WebKit emits notify::uri synchronously inside webkit_web_view_load_uri, before onNavigate is installed; now reported from an idle. AFTER, all three environments (bare, hw, sw): process alive, `navigated to https://example.com/`, RENDER COMPLETE, Example Domain drawn, Navigations reported: 1 (bare and hw log the llvmpipe switch; sw passes the probe untouched). USER NAVIGATION: under XWayland (GDK_BACKEND=x11) an xdotool click on Learn more logged `https://www.iana.org/domains/example`, `http://www.iana.org/help/example-domains`, `https://www.iana.org/help/example-domains` and drew the IANA page -- WebKit reports each redirect hop where WebView2 reports only the final URI. NOT REACHED: input injected from Windows into the Wayland WSLg window (touch tap on the link; tap then Tab+Return with the WSLg window in the foreground) moved nothing in the page, although a touch drag moved the window itself; recorded as a limit of that injection path, which the install script already notes for Wayland."
2026-09-17,wsl,gtk4,P38,ok,ok,window,default,"#P38 on WSL, Wayland: CORRECTING the earlier row of today that said input injected from Windows into the Wayland WSLg window does not reach WebKit. It does. That row was measured on an app whose WebKit web process was already dead: the ad-hoc runs started P38 with nohup from a short-lived wsl.exe, nohup only protects P38 itself, WebKit resets signal dispositions for its children, and WebKitWebProcess/WebKitNetworkProcess were killed when that session ended (ps showed P38 alone). The last frame stayed on screen, so it looked healthy. Started instead with setsid (ps: P38, WebKitNetworkPr, WebKitWebProces all alive after the launching session ended), an input-probe page (file:///tmp/p38probe.html, every event moves location.hash so notify::uri logs it, and the page also prints the event list) recorded, from Windows-injected input in the default Wayland mode: a touch tap on the button -> pointerdown-1, mousedown-2, focus-3, focusin-4, click-5; a Tab key -> keydown-6-Tab. P38 displayed Navigations reported: 6 and the event log has all six. Touch arrives as mouse events (no touchstart). Positive control first: XWayland + xdotool on the same probe logged pointerdown/focus/focusin/click. A second error hid this for a while: the log was read with cut -c32- | grep navigated, and the 31-character timestamp cut took the n, so grep matched nothing (mistakes.md 19). The project harness (test_common.zsh) keeps wsl.exe running for the life of the app and is not affected. Also measured on the way: touch taps and keyboard reach ordinary GTK widgets in WSLg Wayland (P41 calendar tap -> .graphical -> 2025-08-13; three Tabs moved the focus ring to the year button), but a touch tap does not move GTK keyboard focus onto the tapped widget."
2026-09-17,android,android,P11,ok,ok,window,default,"#82 slider drag on Android: the SAME 8dp theft that took pinch and rotate from ContinuousGestureContainer the same day, and it takes the slider too. New file actions/android/P11-drag-the-first-slider.csv -- the existing P11 Android file only CLICKS the sliders, so a drag had never been replayed here. BEFORE: readout still minimum 20 with writes: min 0, uiautomator putting the thumb at 319..445 against a track starting at 368 (the very start, unmoved), and the PAGE scrolled right instead. AFTER requestDisallowInterceptTouchEvent(true) on ACTION_DOWN in CustomSlider: minimum 44, writes: min 8 (one per intermediate move), track 0..1078 with no scroll and the thumb at 378..504. Material BaseSlider does call requestDisallowInterceptTouchEvent itself and it is not enough: its call comes after it decides the drag is horizontal, by which time the HorizontalScrollView root has taken it. Handed back when the drag turns out vertical (dy > dx and dy > slop) so a list under a slider still scrolls. Captures p11-android-final-20260917-151243.png (before) and -151705.png (after)."
2026-09-17,mac,appkit,P50,ok,ok,window,default,"#109 arrowEdge on AppKit: implemented and driven as a pair, and the mapping was inverted on the first attempt. NSPopover takes its edge as an argument to show(relativeTo:of:preferredEdge:) and keeps no property for it, so NSCustomPopover carries the preference and presentPopover spends it. First mapping followed the comment already in the file -- that .maxY means below, reasoned from this backend flipped views -- and the captures said otherwise: p50-macos-final-20260917-155815.png reports arrow edge: top with the panel BELOW, -155948.png reports bottom with it ABOVE. So NSPopover reads preferredEdge in AppKit own y-up screen space. After swapping: -160136.png top with the panel above and the readout visible, -160229.png bottom with it below. The readout is what makes these measurements rather than two pictures of a popover, since AppKit moves a popover that does not fit. ALSO FOUND, and fixed in P50 rather than in the backend: the window opens at the content ideal size, 780x825, and section 2 three buttons were drawn as three-pixel bars with no legible text while every Text around them rendered. Resizing by hand to 780x1020 made them whole; resizing WIDER to 1400x825 did not, so it is height. defaultSize does not settle it (the window is resized to the ideal after first layout) and clearing AppKit saved frame from user defaults changed nothing. P50 now asks for .frame(minHeight: 1000) on AppKit only. The underlying gap -- an ideal height about 150 points short of the height at which the content renders -- is in todo.md for whoever owns layout."
2026-09-17,ios,uikit,P50,ok,ok,window,default,"#109 arrowEdge on UIKit: implemented and driven as a pair, and the drive found that iPhone popovers were not popovers at all. UIKit adapts a popover to a sheet in a horizontally compact size class unless the delegate refuses, and CustomPopover did not: p50-ios-final-20260917-161038.png is the panel filling the screen from the top, anchored to nothing, which made P50 own assertion (each panel must land NEXT TO THE BUTTON THAT OPENED IT) unmeetable on iPhone and left #109 with no side to take. CustomPopover now returns .none from adaptivePresentationStyle(for:traitCollection:). After that: -161314.png shows arrow edge: top with the panel above the button and the arrow pointing down at it, -161400.png shows bottom with the panel below and the arrow up. The .bottom file had been written predicting the two would look alike -- 144 points of room against a 200-point panel -- and the run disproved it: UIKit shrinks a popover to the room it has rather than refusing the side. permittedArrowDirections names where the ARROW is, so the mapping is the opposite side from the Edge: .top -> .down, .bottom -> .up."
2026-09-17,android,android,P50,ok,ok,window,default,"#109 arrowEdge on Android: implemented and driven, and it is the one backend where the side is arithmetic rather than a property. PopupWindow has no placement: showAsDropDown(anchor) means below and every other side is that call with an offset from the anchor and popup pixel sizes, so createPopover now returns CustomPopupWindow (a Kotlin subclass holding the preference) and presentPopover computes the offsets. THE TOP/BOTTOM PAIR DOES NOT DISCRIMINATE HERE AND THAT IS MEASURED: the first anchor is at y 755 in a 914-point window, about 140 points of room against a 200-point panel, and showAsDropDown moves a popup that does not fit below to above the anchor -- p50-android-final-20260917-162100.png (.top) and -162204.png (.bottom) are the same panel in the same place, each with the readout naming a different value. Scrolling the anchor higher is not available: the content ends at y 2391 of 2400 pixels, and a scroll row changed no bounds at all. The side that does discriminate is .trailing: -162438.png has the panel shifted right and level with the button rather than a row above it, with the readout reading trailing. Same arithmetic, different platform answer from iOS, which shrank the panel instead."
2026-09-17,windows,winui,P4,ok,n/a,window,default,"#M8 .inspect trap on WinUIBackend: FIXED and driven. Before: P4-WinUI died at launch, `SwiftCrossUI/AnyWidget.swift:48: Fatal error: AnyWidget used with incompatible widget type TextBox; actual widget type is Canvas`, exit 132 -- TextField renders through TextFieldStyle, so its node's widget is the Canvas container and the TextBox is inside. The typed .inspect overloads now search the subtree (logical children of Panel/Border/ContentControl first, because .onCreate runs before attach when VisualTreeHelper may report nothing; VisualTreeHelper for templated controls) and fatalError with the tree when nothing matches. After: P4 runs, and the closure acted on the real TextBox -- the border colour it sets, RGB(20,70,120), measured exactly on the top (y=258), bottom (y=289) and left (x=21) edges of p4-inspect-fixed-20260917-154041.png. List and NavigationSplitView overloads unchanged and unmeasured: no app calls them."
2026-09-17,windows,gtk4,P4,ok,n/a,window,default,"#M8 .inspect trap on GtkBackend, which the Mac-side queue entry did not list: FIXED and driven. Before: P4-gtk4 died at launch, `Fatal error: AnyWidget used with incompatible widget type Entry; actual widget type is PassthroughFixed`, exit 132, from an .inspect closure that is EMPTY on this backend. The typed overloads now search the Swift-side child records the Gtk module keeps (Fixed.children, Box.children, ScrolledWindow/Viewport child, both Paned sides) and fatalError with the tree when nothing matches. After: P4-gtk4 runs and renders (p4-gtk-inspect-fixed-20260917-154537.png); since a failed search is fatal, surviving launch shows the Entry was found. List and NavigationSplitView overloads unchanged and unmeasured. WSL shares this GtkBackend source and was not run separately."
2026-09-17,wsl,gtk4,P69,ok,n/a,window,default,"#123 GTK, the X child: FIXED and verified externally on WSLg. Baseline reproduced with the repo probe (p69_atspi.zsh): checks label/hint/value/hidden true, no_original_label FALSE, exit 1. A depth-indented AT-SPI dump (scratch; the repo probe prints a flat list) showed `button 'Close' -> panel -> panel -> label 'X'` -- the visible X is a DESCENDANT of the labelled button, and every button has the same shape (`Delete` and `Volume` over labels of the same text). Fix in GtkBackend+Accessibility.swift: when a label is set on a widget whose accessible role is BUTTON, its direct children get GTK_ACCESSIBLE_STATE_HIDDEN, cleared when the label is removed. Both conditions are deliberate: an unlabelled button's name is computed from that content (Delete, Volume), and a label on a container must not hide the controls inside it. After: probe exit 0, all five checks true; the dump shows `button 'Close'` with no children and `Delete`/`Volume` unchanged. Built on WSL and on Windows -gtk4 (0 errors). Not verified: what Orca actually speaks."
2026-09-17,mac,appkit,P50,ok,ok,window,default,"#109 follow-up, AppKit default edge: a popover that asks for nothing now opens BELOW its anchor, as it does on the other four backends. Driven with actions/mac/P50-popover-default-edge.csv, which opens PANEL ALPHA without touching the cycle button so the readout still reads arrow edge: none (platform decides). Before: p50-macos-final-20260917-195515.png, panel ABOVE. After: -195726.png, panel BELOW. The call had carried .maxY since the popover landed, under a comment saying that put the panel below the button -- the intent was right and the claim was wrong, and nothing had ever varied it. GtkPopover default position is below, PopupWindow showAsDropDown means below, WinUI Flyout auto-places below where it fits, UIKit [.up,.down] takes below when there is room; AppKit was the only one placing a no-preference popover above."
2026-09-17,ios,uikit,P50,ok,ok,window,default,"#109 follow-up, UIKit aligned to Android when the requested side does not fit. Android MOVES a popup that does not fit to the other side of the anchor (showAsDropDown) and keeps it whole; UIKit SHRINKS the popover into the room the permitted direction has. P50 showed the cost: p50-ios-final-20260917-161400.png is the panel below its button with press me (0) and close this panel cut off the bottom edge -- present, anchored, obedient and unusable. UIKitBackend+Popover.swift now measures the room against the window safe area (plus 13 points of arrow) and asks for the opposite side when the requested one cannot hold the panel, which is Android rule; UIKit own shrink remains the floor when neither side fits. After: -200019.png, the same bottom request, the panel ABOVE and whole. .top re-driven unchanged: -200123.png. A consequence worth stating: top and bottom now produce the same placement at this anchor on this device, exactly as they do on Android, so the discriminating evidence for iOS is the before/after of the alignment rather than the pair."
2026-09-17,windows,gtk4,P11,ok,n/a,window,default,"#82-shape slider-in-scroll check on GtkBackend, answering the todo question for Windows. P11 --nested-slider (new flag) puts a Slider inside ScrollView(.horizontal) and another inside ScrollView(.vertical). One-finger synthetic touch, touch_gesture.exe drag. RESULT, no steal: horizontal slider dragged 91->460 logged `nested horizontal -> 98` and the container did not move (capture p11g-h-slider-20260917-165425.png); a swipe on the wide text 720->380 scrolled the horizontal container off |H (p11g-h-swipe-...165429.png); a swipe on `tall content 1` upward scrolled the vertical container to rows 2-5 with the vertical slider still 0 (p11g-v-swipe-...165434.png); the vertical slider drag reached 100 in 10/10 runs. FOUND ON THE WAY, a CRASH inside GTK 4.22.4: after the vertical slider drag, `Gdk-CRITICAL gdk_device_get_display: assertion 'GDK_IS_DEVICE (device)' failed` and then an access violation. All 8 WER reports: c0000005 at gtk-4-1.dll+0x4ec0b1 = _gdk_win32_get_cursor_pos (gdkevents-win32.c:166, resolved with GTK's PDB via cdb), called with a NULL display from a freed winpointer device. INTERLEAVED A/B in one harness: device destroyed ~16 ms after UP, 4/6 crashed, critical ~50 ms after the tool exited; destroyed 3 s after UP, 0/6. Source (4.22.4 gdkinput-winpointer.c): WM_POINTERDEVICECHANGE drops the seat and device-manager references while a gesture still holds the raw pointer. Real-hardware equivalent: unplugging a touch screen or tablet, or an RDP session removing its touch device. FIX in GtkCHelpers/gtk_device_lifetime.c: take a reference on the seat's device-removed signal, which fires before both unrefs, and never release it. AFTER, same harness and immediate destroy: 10/10 alive, 0 criticals. Caveat: the before/after runs were sequential builds, not interleaved. A zsh-launched harness never crashed on the old build either (0/21), and under cdb 0/6. The trigger is timing-sensitive, which is why the A/B was run in the bash harness where it reproduced. WSL not driven."
2026-09-17,windows,winui,P11,ok,n/a,window,default,"#82-shape slider-in-scroll check on WinUIBackend, answering the todo question for Windows. P11 --nested-slider (new flag): a Slider inside ScrollView(.horizontal) and another inside ScrollView(.vertical), driven by one-finger synthetic touch (touch_gesture.exe drag). RESULT, no steal: horizontal slider 79->467 wrote 29 times ending `nested horizontal -> 100`; vertical slider 30 writes ending 100. CONTROL, so a container that cannot scroll at all is not mistaken for one that yields: a swipe on the wide text 700->450 scrolled the horizontal container, and a swipe on the tall content upward scrolled the vertical one, both confirmed by eye on p11-nested-control-h-20260917-160004.png and p11-nested-control-v-20260917-160008.png. A pixel-marker script was tried first and its numbers were ambiguous, so the captures are the evidence. Not measured: whether the container moved a few px during the slider drags."
2026-09-17,windows,winui,P69,ok,n/a,window,default,"#123 WinUI UIA tree (todo item) and .accessibilityLabel on Text, EXTERNAL probe. New testapp/test_support/measure/p69_uia.zsh + uia_tree.c dumps UI Automation out of process in a NAMED view. BEFORE: control view 24 nodes and content view 19 both contain `button Close > text X` and `text decorative` -- a real defect, not a raw-view artefact. In-process readback had the hidden Canvas as raw: AccessibilityView applies to one element and UIA promotes its children. FIX in WinUIBackend+Accessibility.swift: raw on the whole subtree (walk shared with .inspect scuiChildren), cleared rather than set to .content when unhidden; a labelled Button gets its content subtree raw. AFTER: control 22 / content 17 nodes, X and decorative absent; probe 14/14 exit 0 (7 checks x 2 views). NEW Text check: P69 Text(12:30).accessibilityLabel(Half past twelve) reads `text Half past twelve` with no 12:30. HelpText and ItemStatus (40 percent) both read. NEGATIVE CONTROL: same probe on P69-gtk4.exe exits 1. FOUND: unlabelled Delete and Volume buttons have an EMPTY UIA Name (text only as child). Narrator speech not heard."
2026-09-17,windows,gtk4,P69,ok,n/a,window,default,"#123 GtkBackend on Windows: NO accessibility backend. p69_uia.zsh on P69-gtk4.exe: the UIA tree is one node, the window (class gdkSurfaceToplevel), nothing inside; probe exit 1. GTK_A11Y=help on the installed gvsbuild bundle prints `accesskit - Disabled during GTK build` and `atspi - Not available on this platform`. GTK 4.22 can expose UIA through AccessKit, but this build omits it, so every .accessibility* modifier is a no-op here. Fix needs a GTK built with AccessKit."
2026-09-17,wsl,gtk4,P69,ok,n/a,window,default,"#123 .accessibilityLabel on Text, GtkBackend, external AT-SPI (p69_atspi.zsh, two new checks text_label / no_original_text). Synced and rebuilt P69 on WSL. Probe exit 0, all 7 checks true: `label Half past twelve`, no node named 12:30; the earlier five still pass."
2026-09-17,windows,winui,P67,ok,n/a,window,default,"#123 WinUI unlabelled buttons had an EMPTY UIA Name (found by p69_uia.zsh: button name empty with help=Removes the file permanently, text Delete only as a child). XAML derives a Button name from content only when content is a string. FIX: ViewLabelCustomButton holds accessibilityLabelOverride and on every updateButton sets Name = override ?? first TextBlock text in its content, hiding the content subtree once named so the words are read once -- the AppKit firstTextFieldValue shape. p69_uia.zsh gained derived_names and no_duplicate_text. BEFORE (previous build): both false in both views. AFTER: 18/18 in control and content views, exit 0. P67 (the name-derivation app, never run on Windows before) dumped in the control view: plain label / padded label / two / empty for the image-only button -- identical to the AppKit and iOS rows of 2026-09-11."
2026-09-17,android,android,P50,ok,ok,window,default,"#109 follow-up on Android, both halves asked for after looking at the captures: the popover now draws a TAIL, and it takes the opposite side the way iOS does instead of relying on Android shifting. (1) THE TAIL: a PopupWindow is a plain window with a background drawable and Android draws no arrow on one, while GTK, AppKit and UIKit all point at their anchor -- which is what says WHICH control a panel belongs to. CustomPopupWindow.kt now sets a PopoverBackground drawable: rounded body plus a triangle on the side facing the anchor, aligned with the anchor centre, in the panel colour (the app colour when it set one, the theme colourBackground otherwise), and getPadding reserves the strip so the tail does not eat a line of content. The popup grows by 8dp on that axis. (2) THE FIT: resolveEdge measures the room against the window insets and returns the opposite side when the requested one cannot hold the panel, which is the same measurement UIKit was given the same day; showAsDropDown own shifting pushes a panel until it clears the screen edge, which can leave it covering the control it was opened from. Driven: p50-android-final-20260917-204127.png (.bottom, no room below, panel above with the tail pointing DOWN at the button), -204238.png (.top, same), -204303.png (.trailing, panel to the right with the tail on its LEFT edge pointing at the anchor). ALSO WORTH RECORDING: the first rebuild was run with --no-build and showed no tail at all; compile.zsh -android compiles but does not package an APK, so test_android.zsh --no-build replayed the previous package. The photo was of a stale build, not of a missing feature."
2026-09-17,windows,winui,P50,ok,ok,window,default,"#109 WinUI, two findings. (1) DEFAULT POPOVER NEVER APPEARED: with no arrowEdge, presentPopover used placement .auto (since dea9ccff), and FlyoutPlacementMode.auto shows nothing and throws nothing; the log still wrote `popover alpha shown`. Out-of-process UIA (uia_tree) found 0 PANEL nodes for PANEL BETA, PANEL ALPHA, and ALPHA cycled back to none; window capture and desktop capture also empty. POSITIVE CONTROL: with .trailing the same dump found PANEL ALPHA. The 2026-09-16 verification drove only set preferences. Experiment: fallback changed to .bottom -> both panels appear; FIX: fallback .bottom, matching the other four backends (the Mac side moved AppKit to below the same day, cf4a4a88, whose comment claims WinUI Flyout auto-places below -- it showed nothing). An intermediate .top build also showed both panels. AFTER (.bottom): beta 1, alpha 1, alpha-after-full-cycle 1, alpha leading 1; p50w-bottomdefault-cycle0-20260917-201908.png has ALPHA below its button (room below) and p50w-bottomdefault-beta-20260917-201856.png has BETA flipped above (no room below). The old comment reason for .auto (a sided value would be honoured with no room) was already refuted on 2026-09-16 (.bottom flipped above). Recorded as mistakes #21. (2) LEADING/TRAILING, the Mac side question: at the default 782-wide window the pair CANNOT differ -- the first button has ~127 px on its left for a ~285 px panel, so .leading flipped right and both captures are identical (p50w-edge-cycle3/-cycle4-20260917-200004/200017). Widened to 1500 (content centres, button at x 479-691): .leading puts the panel LEFT (right edge 473 < button 479), .trailing RIGHT (p50w-wide-edge-cycle3/-cycle4-20260917-200149/200206). Replay: scratch action files plus a scratch SetWindowPos resize during the settle sleep."
2026-09-17,windows,gtk4,P50,ok,ok,window,default,"#109 GtkBackend leading/trailing, the Mac side question, measured as WINDOW RECTS rather than pictures: a GtkPopover is its own gdkSurfaceToplevel sharing no reliable capture, so a scratch EnumWindows tool read both surfaces. Main window pinned at screen x=600 (a GTK popover is constrained by the screen, so the room depends on where the window sits; at x=52 the left side has ~190 px for a 303 px panel). Button on screen x 741-963, centre y 739. Two rounds, identical: leading popover x 441-744 (right edge at button left: LEFT), trailing x 961-1264 (left edge at button right: RIGHT), none x 707-998 from y 754 (BELOW, GTK default). Taps by touch_gesture.exe drag with equal endpoints, because action-file replay fixes the frame origin at launch and every click missed once the window was moved."
2026-09-17,windows,winui,P50,ok,ok,window,default,"#109 WinUI popover had NO ARROW (user report 2026-09-17: There is no arrow). Flyout draws no beak; the file header wrongly listed the beak among what Flyout handles, and TeachingTip (the one WinUI control with a tail) is not projected by swift-winui. FIX: PopoverArrow in WinUIBackend+Popovers.swift. After opened (deferred one main-queue turn), it reads the FlyoutPresenter and anchor rects via transformToVisual, derives the side from where the panel LANDED (so a flipped panel points back correctly), and draws a 20x10 triangle in a separate Popup with two Paths: fill = presenter.background, and a stroke on the slanted sides only = presenter.borderBrush. No arrow when rects overlap or the edge is too short. SPACING: XAML leaves 4 DIP between panel and anchor (SCUI_DEBUG_POPOVER_ARROW=1 rects: panel top 657 vs anchor bottom 653), so the tip sat ON the button. FlyoutShowOptions.exclusionRect alone had no effect (gap still 4); position+exclusionRect fixed the gap but broke flipping (narrow .leading clamped to x=0 over its own button, panel 0-301 vs button 126-339). Kept plain showAt and moved the presenter with translation by depth+3-gap: narrow .leading flips right again (panel at 343). AFTER, contact sheet p50w-popover-arrow-final.png from p50w-arrowfinal-*-20260917-2059xx/2100xx: none -> below, arrow up; BETA no room -> above, arrow down; wide .leading -> left, arrow right; wide .trailing -> right, arrow left. FOUND, NOT caused by this: pressing press me INSIDE the WinUI flyout does not register by action-file click or by touch tap (0/2 each), on this build AND on the pre-arrow build (control), so it predates the arrow."
2026-09-17,windows,winui,P50,ok,ok,window,default,"WinUI: a stroked shape swallowed every click inside its outline -- found as P50 popover press me doing nothing. The user clicked press me for real: no log line. UIA Invoke on the same button logged popover counter 1, so the click wiring was fine and hit testing was not. Cause: renderPath set Path.fill to a TRANSPARENT SolidColorBrush for an invisible fill, XAML hit-tests a transparent brush (null is skipped), and .border is an overlay of Rectangle().stroke(...), laid over the panel via .border(accent, width: 2). FIX in WinUIBackend.swift renderPath: fill = nil when the fill is a colour with opacity 0. BEFORE: touch taps on press me 0/2 on the arrow build and 0/2 on the pre-arrow build (control); action-file clicks 0/3. AFTER: touch taps logged counter 1 and 2; the user then clicked for real and the log reads popover counter 1, 2, 3. Scope: any WinUI .border or stroked shape laid over a control blocked it, not only popovers. Same session, user-driven check of the arrow: after widening the window, ALPHA (anchor x 696-909) opened leading at 391-692, trailing from 913, top above, bottom below, each with the arrow toward the button; at the default width leading flips right. P50 readout now says arrow edge (first panel only), because the tester first cycled the edge and opened BETA, which is the control and ignores it."
2026-09-17,windows,gtk4,P50,ok,ok,window,default,"Follow-up to the WinUI stroked-shape hit-test fix: does GtkBackend have the same defect behind the 2026-09-09 note that the replay cannot press a button inside the popover (P50-press-inside-popover.csv)? NO. Main window pinned at x=600, first panel opened by touch tap; the popover surface (its own toplevel, title P50-gtk4.exe) captured directly as p50g-popover-20260917-213155.png, beak up, press me at popover (68,141). Two touch taps there logged popover counter 1 and 2. GTK hit testing inside a .border-ed panel is fine; the 09-09 failure belongs to the mouse synthesiser path (SetCursorPos + SendInput), not to the stroke overlay. No GtkBackend change."
2026-09-17,wsl,gtk4,P67,ok,n/a,window,default,"#123 P67 on GtkBackend, never run on the Windows track before: AT-SPI tree read externally on WSLg (scratch wsl_p67_tree.zsh + atspi_tree.py, private dbus-run-session). Names: plain label / padded label / two texts / empty for the image-only button. The first, second and fourth match AppKit, iOS and WinUI; the third differs, and GTK is the one closer to SwiftUI (VoiceOver reads both texts), since GTK computes the name from ALL content where AppKit firstTextFieldValue takes the first. GTK keeps the label nodes under each button (button plain label > panel > panel > label plain label), as it did for P69 before the labelled-button fix: an unlabelled button name is computed from those nodes, so they stay."
2026-09-17,windows,winui,P67,ok,n/a,window,default,"#123 P67 WinUI re-run after joining ALL label texts for an unlabelled button (was first text only, copied from AppKit): control view reads plain label / padded label / two texts / empty -- now matching GtkBackend on WSLg. P69 UIA probe still 18/18. P67 on-screen expectation updated to two texts; AppKit/UIKit/Android handed the same change in todo.md."
2026-09-17,mac,appkit,P67,ok,n/a,window,default,"#123 P67 button names on the three backends here, following the Windows handover: a button labelled with two Texts is now named from BOTH of them, joined by a space, not from the first one only. AppKit firstTextFieldValue, UIKit firstText and Android firstText all took the first text; P67 third button therefore announced itself as two, dropping texts from beside it on screen. Three external probes, one per platform, all now read plain label / padded label / two texts / (empty): ax_dump on macOS, uiautomator dump --compressed on emulator-5554, and test_ios.zsh --dump-tree for the XCUITest tree (new file actions/ios/P67-read-the-tree.csv). That matches GtkBackend read over AT-SPI, WinUI read over UIA, SwiftUI VoiceOver, and P67 own on-screen expectation, which Windows had already changed to two texts. .accessibilityLabel(_:) still overrides the derived name."
2026-09-17,ios,uikit,P67,ok,ok,window,default,"#123 P67 button names on the three backends here, following the Windows handover: a button labelled with two Texts is now named from BOTH of them, joined by a space, not from the first one only. AppKit firstTextFieldValue, UIKit firstText and Android firstText all took the first text; P67 third button therefore announced itself as two, dropping texts from beside it on screen. Three external probes, one per platform, all now read plain label / padded label / two texts / (empty): ax_dump on macOS, uiautomator dump --compressed on emulator-5554, and test_ios.zsh --dump-tree for the XCUITest tree (new file actions/ios/P67-read-the-tree.csv). That matches GtkBackend read over AT-SPI, WinUI read over UIA, SwiftUI VoiceOver, and P67 own on-screen expectation, which Windows had already changed to two texts. .accessibilityLabel(_:) still overrides the derived name."
2026-09-17,android,android,P67,ok,n/a,window,default,"#123 P67 button names on the three backends here, following the Windows handover: a button labelled with two Texts is now named from BOTH of them, joined by a space, not from the first one only. AppKit firstTextFieldValue, UIKit firstText and Android firstText all took the first text; P67 third button therefore announced itself as two, dropping texts from beside it on screen. Three external probes, one per platform, all now read plain label / padded label / two texts / (empty): ax_dump on macOS, uiautomator dump --compressed on emulator-5554, and test_ios.zsh --dump-tree for the XCUITest tree (new file actions/ios/P67-read-the-tree.csv). That matches GtkBackend read over AT-SPI, WinUI read over UIA, SwiftUI VoiceOver, and P67 own on-screen expectation, which Windows had already changed to two texts. .accessibilityLabel(_:) still overrides the derived name."
2026-09-17,windows,winui,P54,ok,n/a,window,default,"Verification of the 2026-09-09 Windows in-flight table (over 7 days old). setRefreshHandler(to: nil): P54 gained opt-in --toggle-refreshable, a button above the scroll view that passes a nil onRefresh environment value to the SAME ScrollView (an if around it would rebuild the widget and never reach the nil path). Six toggles driven through UIA Invoke: Refresh buttons 0/1 alternating, never 2 (a nested Grid would add one); after the third re-enable, Invoke on Refresh moved refreshes 0 -> 1. Task #48 in the four files the table named: bare try? replaced by do/catch + DebugFeatures.log in Alerts getResults, AngularGradient invalidate, Button clearValue x4, ButtonPressState registration, plus a clearValue in Accessibility added the same day; the remaining try? (pixelBuffer.buffer) already logs via logger.warning. Regression after that change: P69 UIA probe 18/18, P54 refresh as above, P50 press me inside the popover by touch -> (1), P31 alert opened then OK handler logged `alert OK` with no getResults failure."
2026-09-18,windows,winui,P4,ok,n/a,window,default,"queue M8 follow-up, the two .inspect overloads nobody had run: List and NavigationSplitView, on both Windows backends, driven by the new opt-in P4 --inspect-containers. TWO THINGS ARE NEEDED to reach the specific overload, both measured: .inspect must come BEFORE .frame (the overloads are declared on List / NavigationSplitView, so after a modifier only the generic View.inspect applies), and the closure parameter type must be written out. With the frame first and no annotation, WinUI reported `Canvas` three times -- the generic overload -- and the annotated version did not compile. WinUI, once reached: List -> CustomListView, NavigationSplitView -> CustomSplitView, no trap. GTK: `Could not cast value of type Gtk.ListView to Gtk.ListBox` at launch, exit before the window. Cause: #117 replaced the list box with GTK native lazy GtkListView, while the overload still force-cast `scrolled.getChild() as! Gtk.ListBox` under a comment saying the contract had not changed -- it could not be honoured, there is no list box. FIXED: both GTK overloads now use the M8 search (Gtk.ListView, Gtk.Paned). After: List -> ListView, NavigationSplitView -> CustomPaned, app alive. SOURCE BREAK on GtkBackend for any caller that wrote (Gtk.ListBox) -> Void."
2026-09-18,windows,gtk4,P4,ok,n/a,window,default,"#120 re-checked by RUNNING it, not by reading todo.md: SCUI_HOST_BACKENDS_ONLY=1 swift test with the separate scratch path, filter GridLayoutTests. 12 tests in 2 suites, all passed, with the one known issue still recorded: `A static cell beside a ForEach collapses it into one cell`, mixed 68.0 vs all-ForEach 42.0. Status unchanged since 2026-09-09 and still pinned deliberately (the attempted fix is described in the test: ForEachViewChildren.layoutableChildren is filled during the ForEach own layout, so a parent asking first gets an empty answer and drops the ForEach entirely). Also here: the GTK half of the .inspect check, see the winui P4 row of the same day -- GtkBackend List.inspect trapped at launch (Could not cast Gtk.ListView to Gtk.ListBox) and both GTK overloads were changed to the M8 search; after, List -> ListView and NavigationSplitView -> CustomPaned."
2026-09-17,mac,appkit,P69,ok,n/a,window,default,"#123 the other half of the Windows question: does .accessibilityLabel on a Text reach the text? On AppKit it did NOT, and the probe could not see that until it was widened. ax_dump printed only an AXStaticText value, so P69 read as 12:30 with no sign of the label; printing the description too gave AXStaticText 12:30 desc=Half past twelve -- the label had arrived and the words on screen were still sitting in the attribute a reader takes for static text. GTK over AT-SPI and WinUI over UIA both report the label alone, and P69 own expectation says 12:30 does not appear, so AppKit was the odd one out. FIRST FIX FAILED SILENTLY: setAccessibilityValue(_:) on an NSTextField is accepted and ignored -- the call ran twelve times, printed from inside the branch, and the tree still said 12:30, because a control value comes from its cell. NSCustomTextField now holds an accessibilityValueOverride and answers with it, the same shape NSCustomButton already uses for its label. After: AXStaticText Half past twelve desc=Half past twelve, and 12:30 appears nowhere but inside P69 own claim text."
2026-09-17,android,android,P69,ok,n/a,window,default,"#123 the same question on Android: .accessibilityLabel on a Text reaches it and needed no change. uiautomator dump --compressed on emulator-5554 gives TextView text=12:30 content-desc=Half past twelve. The nodes text attribute keeps the visible string because that is what Android always reports for a TextView -- it is what a sighted user reads -- while contentDescription is what an assistive technology announces in its place. So the GTK/WinUI wording (12:30 does not appear) cannot be met literally on this platform without blanking the text on screen; the equivalent statement here is that the announced name is the label. Not yet verified by a screen reader actually speaking: TalkBack IS installed on this emulator (com.google.android.marvin.talkback), so a real AT reading is available and has not been taken."
2026-09-18,ios,uikit,P50,ok,ok,window,default,"P50 section 2 assertion, readable on iOS for the first time: PANEL BETA lands beside its OWN button. Until the adaptive-presentation fix earlier the same day, every iPhone popover was a sheet covering the screen from the top, so each panel must land NEXT TO THE BUTTON THAT OPENED IT and the two must not land in the same place had nothing to be true or false about. New file actions/ios/P50-beta-panel.csv opens BETA from the second anchor, which carries no arrowEdge and is therefore the control for the #109 work on ALPHA. Capture p50-ios-final-20260918-042521.png: the panel is anchored to the second button with its tail pointing down at it, in a different place from ALPHA. ALSO: the first attempt tapped y 889 and opened nothing, which looks exactly like a popover that failed. The coordinate came from a capture taken before the Windows side reworded the readout to arrow edge (first panel only), and every row below that line had moved; re-measured at y 911 from a capture of the current build."
2026-09-18,mac,appkit,P50,ok,ok,window,default,"AppKit P50 window height, root-caused and fixed in shared code rather than worked around. The window is sized max(minimumWindowSize, proposedWindowSize) where minimumWindowSize is the content laid out at a proposal of .zero. Button.computeLayout passed the proposed height minus padding down to its label, so at a zero proposal the label answered with nothing and the button shrank to its padding: the new SCUI_DEBUG_WINDOW_SIZE=1 instrument reported minimum (82, 797) for P50, about 150 points short of the height the same content draws at, and the window opened exactly there with three buttons rendered as three-pixel bars. Fix: the height is no longer proposed to the label (childProposal.height = nil), because a buttons height on every backend here is its labels height plus padding and none of them stretch or compress one vertically. P50 .frame(minHeight: 1000) workaround REMOVED and the buttons render at 780x985 with no workaround; P52 (96 buttons in three arms) and iOS P50 both re-captured and unchanged in shape. SECOND FINDING, which had masked the first: AppKit createWindow calls setFrameAutosaveName, and the restored frame overrides .defaultSize -- raising defaultSize from 700 to 900 changed nothing until defaults delete P50, so any window-size measurement has to clear that domain first. Shared-code change: GTK and WinUI should have their button heights looked at."
2026-09-18,mac,appkit,P52,ok,n/a,window,default,"P52 re-captured after the Button layout change (childProposal.height = nil): three arms, 48 + 48 buttons and the zero-button control, every label legible and the grid unchanged. Capture p52-macos-final-20260918-043451.png. It differs from the 2026-09-10 one only by appearance (that capture was light mode, this one dark)."
2026-09-18,windows,winui,P50,ok,n/a,window,default,"Shared Button.computeLayout change (f751f7b8, Mac side) verified on Windows, and completed here. BEFORE, with only that change: 8 of the 23 apps in window-sizes-winui-sta-20260917.csv2 opened far taller (P50 782x918 -> 782x2198, P70 592 -> 1282, P23 652 -> 1141, P34 661 -> 1109, P36 732 -> 1053, P5 412 -> 1106, P61 452 -> 805, P11 652 -> 730). GTK the same: P50 808x1005 -> 808x1228, P34 715 -> 819, P36 870 -> 904, P5 448 -> 485, P70 628 -> 725. CAUSE: a window opens at max(minimumWindowSize, proposedWindowSize) and the minimum is the content at .zero; with no height proposed to the label, Text at width 0 with no height limit puts one character per line (its own doc calls this case out). P50 five button labels hold 110 characters, and 110 lines is the 1280 points it grew. Confirmed by reverting the single line locally: P50 back to 932, P23 652, P5 412. FIX, on top of the Mac change rather than instead of it: at a zero-WIDTH probe the label is proposed no width either, so a button minimum is its label ideal size plus padding. AFTER, full 23-app sweep: 20 unchanged; P36 732->797 and P50 918->932 are NOT from this (both measure the same with the change fully reverted -- P36 changed in 9abe754f, P50 in today Mac edits), leaving P5 482x412 -> 526x412 as the only real effect: minimum WIDTH now fits the widest button. GTK, 7 apps: every height back to its pre-change value, P5 width 508 -> 582. Test suite 101 tests, 19 suites, passed with the one known issue (#120). New baseline testapp/measurements/window-sizes-winui-20260918.csv2. NOT re-verified here: AppKit and UIKit, which the Mac side drove."
2026-09-18,mac,appkit,P5,ok,n/a,window,default,"Re-verification asked for by the Windows side after a0f1e94c (propose the label no width at a zero-width probe): does the minimum width grow on AppKit and UIKit? Measured on one machine with SCUI_DEBUG_WINDOW_SIZE=1, defaults delete before every launch (AppKit restores a saved frame over .defaultSize), and a rebuild from f751f7b8~1 Button.swift as the before. APPKIT: P5 480x408 -> 551x408, minimum 120x121 -> 551x166, because its .defaultSize is 480x380 and the new minimum width now sets the window width -- the same direction as WinUI 482->526 and GTK 508->582. P50 780x928 unchanged (minimum 82x374 -> 299x434, both still under the proposal). P52 1000x928 unchanged, minimum 724x0 both times. Heights unchanged on all three. UIKIT: P50 and P52 pixel-identical apart from the status bar clock and home indicator; P5 changed and not for the better -- a phone window cannot grow, so the width shows up as clipping: the three alert buttons used to wrap to two lines each and fit in the 440-point window, and now each label is one line, the row is wider than the window and cut at both edges. Full table in testapp/measurements/window-sizes-appkit-20260918.csv2. Also corrected: yesterday I wrote that P50 minimum height was 797; that number was inferred from a window size, and the instrument says 374 at that revision. The squeeze itself is real and still demonstrated -- forcing the window to 780x825 today, the buttons stay legible where they were three-pixel bars before the fix."
2026-09-18,ios,uikit,P5,ok,n/a,window,default,"Re-verification asked for by the Windows side after a0f1e94c (propose the label no width at a zero-width probe): does the minimum width grow on AppKit and UIKit? Measured on one machine with SCUI_DEBUG_WINDOW_SIZE=1, defaults delete before every launch (AppKit restores a saved frame over .defaultSize), and a rebuild from f751f7b8~1 Button.swift as the before. APPKIT: P5 480x408 -> 551x408, minimum 120x121 -> 551x166, because its .defaultSize is 480x380 and the new minimum width now sets the window width -- the same direction as WinUI 482->526 and GTK 508->582. P50 780x928 unchanged (minimum 82x374 -> 299x434, both still under the proposal). P52 1000x928 unchanged, minimum 724x0 both times. Heights unchanged on all three. UIKIT: P50 and P52 pixel-identical apart from the status bar clock and home indicator; P5 changed and not for the better -- a phone window cannot grow, so the width shows up as clipping: the three alert buttons used to wrap to two lines each and fit in the 440-point window, and now each label is one line, the row is wider than the window and cut at both edges. Full table in testapp/measurements/window-sizes-appkit-20260918.csv2. Also corrected: yesterday I wrote that P50 minimum height was 797; that number was inferred from a window size, and the instrument says 374 at that revision. The squeeze itself is real and still demonstrated -- forcing the window to 780x825 today, the buttons stay legible where they were three-pixel bars before the fix."
2026-09-18,mac,appkit,P36,ok,n/a,window,default,"Cross-checked the Windows 23-app window-size table against AppKit, after reading it on Ralic-W11 over multissh: same HEAD 3cbf45a9, clean tree, and the measurements directory holds only the three tables that are already in the repo -- so the claim that the table was not on this side was wrong, it was committed. Diffing their two tables (sta-20260917 before, 20260918 after both Button changes): 3 of 23 apps moved -- P5 width 482->526, P36 height 732->797, P50 height 918->932 -- and 20 are identical. The same three on AppKit: P5 480x408 -> 551x408 (+71 width), P36 820x728 unchanged (its minimum height does rise 466 -> 481, the buttons no longer collapsing, but that stays far under the 820x700 proposal), P50 780x928 unchanged. So the width growth is shared by both desktop backends, while the height growth appears only where an app window was already sitting at its minimum -- which is the WinUI case for P36 and not the AppKit one. P50s +14 on WinUI is not safely attributable to the Button change either: its content was edited on both sides today."
2026-09-18,ios,uikit,P5,ok,n/a,window,default,"Windows asked whether the minimum-width change could be limited to mobile. Done, but the condition is not the operating system: it is whether THIS window can grow. New internal environment value windowSizeIsFixed, set by WindowReference from isWindowProgrammaticallyResizable(_:) -- the same answer it already uses for windowSizeIsFinal, so there is one fact read in one place rather than a second guess at which platforms are phones. Button.computeLayout keeps the OLD proposal when the flag is true (both axes minus padding, so the label may be compressed and wraps) and takes the new rule when it is false. iOS P5 is back to its pre-change layout: the blue button text occupies rows 1474..1788 and columns 74..1234, exactly its extent before either Button change, where the clipped build had a single line at 1508..1754; 3209 pixels differ from the pre-change capture and none by more than 60/255, which is antialiasing. Android P5 wraps and fits with nothing clipped (AndroidBackend answers false, same path as UIKit). AppKit is untouched by the carve-out: P5 551x408 with minimum 551x166, P50 780x928 with minimum 299x434. For the Windows side: GTK answers false when a window is FULLSCREEN, so a fullscreen GTK window now takes the compressible path too -- consistent with the rule, worth one look; WinUI always answers true and is unaffected."
2026-09-18,android,android,P5,ok,n/a,window,default,"Windows asked whether the minimum-width change could be limited to mobile. Done, but the condition is not the operating system: it is whether THIS window can grow. New internal environment value windowSizeIsFixed, set by WindowReference from isWindowProgrammaticallyResizable(_:) -- the same answer it already uses for windowSizeIsFinal, so there is one fact read in one place rather than a second guess at which platforms are phones. Button.computeLayout keeps the OLD proposal when the flag is true (both axes minus padding, so the label may be compressed and wraps) and takes the new rule when it is false. iOS P5 is back to its pre-change layout: the blue button text occupies rows 1474..1788 and columns 74..1234, exactly its extent before either Button change, where the clipped build had a single line at 1508..1754; 3209 pixels differ from the pre-change capture and none by more than 60/255, which is antialiasing. Android P5 wraps and fits with nothing clipped (AndroidBackend answers false, same path as UIKit). AppKit is untouched by the carve-out: P5 551x408 with minimum 551x166, P50 780x928 with minimum 299x434. For the Windows side: GTK answers false when a window is FULLSCREEN, so a fullscreen GTK window now takes the compressible path too -- consistent with the rule, worth one look; WinUI always answers true and is unaffected."
2026-09-18,windows,gtk4,P52,ok,n/a,window,default,"P52 on both Windows backends after the shared Button.computeLayout change (item 6 of the Windows list). WinUI: correct -- all three arms draw their 48 buttons as full-height cells with the zero-button control arm present, window 1002x932, RENDER COMPLETE and a two-round benchmark (fixed overhead 327us n=10), capture p52w-buttonfix-20260918-144331.png. GTK on Windows: the app RUNS (RENDER COMPLETE, results printed, fixed overhead 282us) but its WINDOW OPENS COLLAPSED at 752x68 -- title bar and nothing else -- against .defaultSize(width: 1000, height: 900). NOT from the Button change: identical 752x68 with Button.swift reverted to f751f7b8^. Not content-volume: same with --buttons=4 and 48. Not the benchmark: 752x68 already at t+3s, before the 2000ms warm-up ends, and unchanged at t+30s. Not ScrollView-as-root in general: P51 948x928 and P54 748x688, both ScrollView roots, are fine. Resized by hand to 1000x900 the content is correct (p52g-resized-20260918-145444.png): three arms, the control arm, and the results block. GtkBackend never logs its `content size: requested ... allocated ...` line for P52, and that log is guarded by allocated height > 0, so the content was allocated ZERO height. Recorded for follow-up; not chased further today."
2026-09-18,windows,gtk4,P52,ok,ok,window,default,"FIXED: P52 window opened collapsed at 752x68 on GtkBackend. CAUSE, found with SCUI_DEBUG_WINDOW_SIZE=1: pass 1 proposed 1000x900 (the .defaultSize), pass 2 proposed 0x0, pass 3 proposed 724x0, and the window settled at max(minimum 724x0, 0x0). The 0x0 comes from GtkBackend.size(ofWindow:) -> CustomRootWidget.getSize -> gtk_custom_root_widget_get_size, which answers 0x0 until has_been_allocated is true -- and gtk_custom_root_widget_preempt_allocated_size, the call setSize(ofWindow:) makes to record the size just requested, wrote the two numbers and left that flag false. So any window update triggered before GTK first allocates proposes zero. P52 triggers one immediately (its benchmark); P51 and P54 have the same ScrollView root, no early update, and were unaffected; WinUI proposed 1000x900 on all three passes. FIX: preempt sets has_been_allocated. AFTER: P52 1028x968 with every arm drawn (p52g-fixed-20260918-150841.png) and all three passes proposing 1000x900; on WSLg the same three passes propose 1000x900. NO REGRESSION in the other GTK windows measured before and after: P50 808x1005, P51 948x928, P54 748x688, P11 788x688, P23 848x688, all identical. Test suite 101 tests, 19 suites, passed with the known issue."
2026-09-18,mac,appkit,P5,ok,ok,window,default,"#675 on macOS driven end to end for the first time, after finding that the file had never dismissed anything. Its own header warned that an NSAlert maps Escape to a button titled Cancel and that P5 might not have one; driven 2026-09-18 that is exactly what happened -- the alert came up reading Alert A (Secondary) with one OK button, every escape row did nothing, the app sat modal, the three later clicks landed on a window that could not receive them, and P5 own log held one line. The capture looked calm because the harness photographs the main window and the modal was on the secondary one; the only earlier macOS record, 2026-09-01, says no marker configured, capture is timed, so nothing had ever checked. Fixed by using return rather than escape: NSAlert gives Return to its first button and P5 alerts have exactly one. After: the log reads showing Alert A / Alert A dismissed / requesting A, B and C together / Alert A dismissed / Alert B dismissed / Alert C dismissed -- which is the stacking half of #675 in six lines. NOT caused by the Button layout work of the same day: the coordinates still land, and the main-window buttons measured off the new 551x408 window sit under every click the file makes."
2026-09-18,mac,appkit,P27,ok,n/a,window,default,"Swept all 72 built macOS apps with the new SCUI_DEBUG_WINDOW_SIZE instrument, through a new tool: testapp/window_sizes_mac.zsh, the AppKit counterpart to window_sizes.zsh. It prints window / proposed / minimum per app and flags the ones whose window is set by their minimum, and it runs defaults delete before each launch because AppKit restores a saved frame over .defaultSize. Table: testapp/measurements/window-sizes-appkit-sweep-20260918.csv2. Two flagged of 72. P5 width set by the minimum, which is the known consequence of the 2026-09-17 Button change. P27 height set by the minimum AND the framework complaining: .defaultSize(760,520) is smaller than the content minimum of 539, so the window opened at 539 and SwiftCrossUI printed vertical stack ran out of space: 8 children were offered 503 and took 533, at least one child was offered zero -- with the web view at the bottom cut by the window edge. P27 has NO buttons, so this is not the Button work; the number had been too small for a while and nothing had looked. Raised to 620: window 760x648, zero warnings. A third number that looked wrong is not: P53 opens 720x600 against a 548 proposal because it has a toolbar, and the extra 52 points are window chrome. Also recorded rather than acted on: 8 apps report a minimum HEIGHT of zero (P21 P22 P46 P47 P48 P51 P52 P54), content that can collapse entirely, usually a scroll view."
2026-09-18,mac,appkit,P46,ok,n/a,window,default,"Checked the eight apps whose minimum HEIGHT is zero (P21 P22 P46 P47 P48 P51 P52 P54) rather than leaving them as a note: no fix required. Seven contain a ScrollView (P51 three, P54 five), which is the legitimate source of a zero minimum. P46 is the only one with neither ScrollView nor List, so it was the one worth looking at. The test was to force each window to 600x300 and read both the screen and stderr: all eight printed ZERO ""ran out of space"" warnings and zero SwiftCrossUI warnings of any kind, and the captures show content CLIPPED by the window (P46 keeps a scrollbar, P52 has its text cut at the bottom edge) rather than squeezed -- buttons, steppers and gauges keep their normal size. Positive control, so the zero means something: the same warning does fire, on P27 the same day, reading 8 children were offered 503 and took 533. A zero minimum height on these apps means the window may be made small and the content is clipped, not that anything collapses."
2026-09-18,wsl,gtk4,P11,ok,n/a,window,default,"WSL half of two Windows items. (1) #82-shape slider-in-scroll on WSLg, the格 left empty when the two Windows backends were driven: P11 --nested-slider under GDK_BACKEND=x11 (WSLg has no synthetic touch device, so xdotool drives a MOUSE drag; a GtkScrolledWindow pans on TOUCH, so this answers only the slider half and the container-steal half stays the Windows measurement). Coordinates read off a capture taken on WSLg itself (760x710 window, knobs at 79,132 and 194,205), because the Windows layout differs. Horizontal container: nested horizontal -> 99. Vertical container: nested vertical -> 97. App alive after both. No steal. (2) The List and NavigationSplitView .inspect overloads fixed the same day on GtkBackend, driven on WSLg with P4 --inspect-containers: List -> ListView, NavigationSplitView -> CustomPaned, app alive -- identical to Windows -gtk4, which is the point, since the fix is in shared GtkBackend code. Tooling note: xdotool, x11-apps and imagemagick were installed in the distro for this; and the rsync into WSL silently did nothing at first, because the exclude patterns were globbed by the Linux shell when passed through `wsl.exe --`, so they now live in a script file."
2026-09-18,windows,gtk4,P69,ok,n/a,window,default,"GtkBackend on Windows HAS accessibility now: GTK 4.22.4 rebuilt with -Daccesskit=enabled into C:/gtk4-accesskit, recipe committed as testapp/install_gtk4_accesskit_windows.zsh. GTK_A11Y=help on it reads `accesskit - Use the AccessKit accessibility backend` where the gvsbuild bundle read `Disabled during GTK build`, and an out-of-process UIA dump of P69 now holds the whole tree (GtkApplicationWindow > GtkLabel/GtkButton nodes) where it held ONE node, the window. FIVE things had to be worked out, each recorded in the script: vcvars dumped once and read in 8.3 form (Git Bash escapes the quotes a spaced path needs); the bundle pkgconf instead of the pkg-config on PATH (which splits its search path on : and loses every .pc, sending meson into the wrap subprojects); C:/gtk4/bin on PATH for the bundle TOOLS (glib-compile-resources, else the glib subproject is pulled in and collides); clang-cl instead of cl (MSVC 19.44 dies with C1060 out of heap on the 7.3 MB generated gtkresources.c, with ccache, -Zc:preprocessor and the optimisation level ruled out first); and two flags clang-cl needs that GTK only gives msvc -- -D_USE_MATH_DEFINES (M_LN2 in gsk) and -Df16c=disabled (the F16C path uses GNU ifunc, which COFF has not). One local source patch: gtk/gtksvg.c includes <tgmath.h>, which clang-cl cannot reconcile with the MSVC CRT complex types; <math.h> serves it. PROBE, p69_uia.zsh made class-agnostic so it grades both backends (WinUI still 18/18): GTK control view passes label (Close named, no X child), hidden (no decorative), derived_names (Delete and Volume named from their content) -- and FAILS hint, value, text_label, no_original_text and no_duplicate_text. So AccessKit maps the accessible NAME and the hidden state, while GTK description -> UIA HelpText, value-text -> ItemStatus, and a labelled Text (the .accessibilityLabel on Text reads 12:30, not Half past twelve) do not arrive. Those gaps are the next question; the tree existing at all is the change."
2026-09-18,windows,gtk4,P50,ok,n/a,window,default,"Fullscreen check asked for by the Mac side: a fullscreen GtkBackend window now takes the compressible path, since isWindowProgrammaticallyResizable answers false while fullscreen. Driven two ways. (1) P50 put full-monitor borderless through Win32: content centred, buttons at normal height, window updates proposed 1892x1012, nothing squeezed (p50g-fullscreen-20260918-160526.png). That does not prove GDK considered itself fullscreen, so (2) the path was forced: isWindowProgrammaticallyResizable patched to return false locally, P50 and P5 rebuilt. P50 808x1005, unchanged. P5 508x448 against 582x448 on the growable path -- the minimum width stays small, which is the point of the branch -- and its three alert buttons WRAP rather than clip (Show Alert B (stacks on A) on two lines, fully legible, p5g-compressible-20260918-161156.png). The patch was reverted; nothing in the tree carries it."
2026-09-19,windows,gtk4,P69,ok,n/a,window,default,"GTK + AccessKit graded properly: 2 of the 4 failures were the PROBE. uia_tree.c now prints FullDescription and the Value pattern beside HelpText and ItemStatus, because the same idea lives in different UIA properties per toolkit: WinUI writes AutomationProperties.HelpText/ItemStatus while GTK through AccessKit sends description/value. With that, the GTK dump reads `button name=Delete fulldesc=Removes the file permanently` and `button name=Volume value=40 percent` -- both were arriving all along and the probe was reading the WinUI spelling only, the same shape as the earlier raw/control view mix-up. Third: no_duplicate_text was asking GTK to do something it must not, since an unlabelled button name is COMPUTED from its content there (hiding it would leave Delete and Volume nameless), so the check now applies to a button the app labelled, which both backends satisfy. AFTER: WinUI 18/18 unchanged; GTK 14 of 18, the four failures being text_label and no_original_text in both views -- one real gap, and not ours: a Text carrying .accessibilityLabel reads 12:30 through AccessKit and Half past twelve on AT-SPI, because gtkaccesskitcontext.c maps the property to accesskit_node_set_label and then attaches the Pango layout as text runs, and the node reaching UIA is named from the runs. Recorded in todo-Gtk.md for upstream, with nothing to change in GtkBackend."
2026-09-19,windows,gtk4,P69,ok,n/a,window,default,"Windows -gtk4 apps now load the AccessKit runtime by default. One resolver, testapp/test_support/gtk_runtime.zsh, answers which GTK a -gtk4 app loads, and run.zsh, window_sizes.zsh and test_support/test_common.zsh all source it (they each had their own copy, and each had made the same C:/gtk4/bin-is-two-PATH-entries mistake once). It puts C:/gtk4-accesskit/bin first and keeps C:/gtk4/bin behind it, because the AccessKit prefix holds GTK only and glib/pango/cairo/graphene still come from the bundle. NO REBUILD: both are GTK 4.22.4 with the same ABI and an executable built against the bundle headers runs on either DLL. Verified before switching: P50 808x1005, P11 788x688 and P52 1028x968 under the new runtime, identical to the values measured under the old one the same day, and P50 capture p50g-accesskit-20260919-200330.png shows the window drawn correctly with no GSK warning (the new build is clang-cl with f16c and sysprof disabled, so this was worth looking at). After the switch, run.zsh with GTK_A11Y=help reads `accesskit - Use the AccessKit accessibility backend`, and SCUI_GTK_NO_ACCESSKIT=1 reads `Disabled during GTK build` -- the opt-out works, and GTK4_PREFIX still pins one runtime outright. NOT PASSING, and not from this: test_P69.zsh stops at `Renderer preflight failed: -render hw expected D3D12, got nothing` on BOTH runtimes; this host is a remote-desktop session with no hardware adapter, which its own logs have been reporting all along."
